# The Dark Ages: an agent-played game of Dynasty. Scenario and steering: scenario.md; deck: seed.md.
# Every say and aside is the reply of the agent playing that seat (see log/yearN/).
# The Storyteller writes the act lines and refs the moves; the engine keeps the books.
# The narrator's lines are not part of the game: they live in narration.md, never here.
# Each chapter opens with a "# ===== CHAPTER N: <title>" marker.
setup([1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8], "dark-ages", "The Dark Ages",
      "Six players · fourteen years · four years of Chaos, then every work built",
      "Six players: the Emperor, the Official, the Gentry, the Silk and Rice Peasants, and the Warlord. "
      "Reina taxes half of every harvest to build before the population comes, and in Year 2 the whole table revolts. "
      "For four years there is no Emperor: no tax, no works, no land, and an acclamation that splits three ways between "
      "Clark's friends, Rich's grain and Rob's soldiers, while the Warlord carries pieces off into the desert. When the "
      "population doubles in Year 6 and nobody can buy land without a crown, the table acclaims Clark. He builds a work "
      "every year from then on, the whole Great Wall among them, and in Year 14 the Marble Pagoda goes up and the dynasty survives.",
      short="6 players · 14 years · survival")
REVOLT_LINE = True   # briefs.py puts the causes for revolt on every board card (the Director's choice for this game)

# ============================== SETUP ==============================
R(0, "Setup", None, "Six players: the Emperor, the Official, the Gentry, the Silk and Rice Peasants and the Warlord (rulebook Section 4 and Appendix A). No Scholar, so the Emperor appoints someone to read I Ching and count the population, and pays the appointee whatever she chooses (Note 3). Each realia province holds two pieces of its own realia, and each collector needs one from each of the other three. The Warlord starts with two soldiers and may hire one more, for 20, after any march (Section 14).")
act("Six booklets: Reina Osorio draws the Emperor, Clark Grafton the Official, Richard Baron the Gentry, Silke Weber the Silk Peasant, Paddy Doyle the Rice Peasant and Robert Land the Warlord.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
deal_grain("Rich, the Gentry, sets out the Harvest Pile and deals 60 to each player. It is the first year's harvest.")
take_stock("Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy each take two pieces of their own realia out of the box. They wait beside the board until they're sold.")
act("Clark cuts out the Province 2 Irrigation System, one square by two; Rich the Province 3 Reservoir, two by three. They're set aside until built. Nobody sits in Provinces 4 or 7, so nothing is owed there.", map_hl=[2,3])
# ===== CHAPTER 1: Heaven's Own Share

place_army({"W1":"G11", "W2":"Q26"}, "Rob, the Warlord, stands his two soldiers: W1 on G11, on the strip below the Wall where Clark's Province 2 meets Rich's Province 3, and W2 on Q26, in the sea off Silke's Province 5 and Paddy's Province 6. He refuses to pay the crown's tax.")
say("Rob", "Good evening, neighbours. One of my lads is camped under the Wall right where Clark's fields meet Rich's, and the other's on a boat off the coast, just about where Silke's silk meets Paddy's rice. No tax from me, Reina. Collect it yourself if you like, I'll have the kettle on.",
 "Rob: G11 touches Clark's and Rich's land at once, and Q26 out at sea touches both peasants, so each soldier is one step from two of the four pieces I need. Refusing the tax on day one tells the Empress early that she doesn't control me.")
act("Reina, the Emperor, will read the I Ching herself, unpaid, and shuffles the deck.", map_hl=[1])
say("Reina", "The Mandate comes from Heaven, so the crown will read Heaven's book itself. I'll shuffle the deck, turn the cards and keep the count, and nobody will need to wonder whose hand turned them. The first harvest will be a full one, so sharpen your sickles. The walls won't build themselves.",
 "Reina: There's no reason to pay someone grain to read a card I can read myself. Every grain the crown keeps goes to the 890 in works, and I want the foundations dug before the population card turns.")
# ============================== YEAR 1 ==============================
R(1, "The first year", None, "No card is drawn. The 60 each player was dealt is the first harvest, and the tax is levied on it.")
CUR_INC.update({s:60 for s in ACTIVE})
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, what a gracious thing, reading Heaven's book with your own hands. Nobody at this table could doubt a count you keep. Silke, Paddy, my dears, it's the customary five each, the smallest rent in the empire, and the Pile pays out the moment the sickles are in. And Rob, keep your kettle, and keep your boys off my side of the Wall.",
 "Rich: Five is a starting price. Ask too much in Year 1 and the peasants start talking about revolts, so I ask the minimum, look generous, and raise it later when they owe me a favour. My grain is the same as everyone's for now, and I'm watching Clark's Customs House, which I need, and Rob's soldier sitting right on my border.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum rent, and each pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "Half the harvest, friends. The fields will never be fuller than they are tonight, and an empire that waits for lean years to build will never build at all. The Summer Palace goes up this year, and the Wall and the Canal start from it. Clark, collect it cleanly and you'll have your five at year's end. Rob, keep your kettle. The crown doesn't knock on tents.",
 "Reina: At 50% the crown takes 120, which leaves the reserve at 155 after the Palace and Clark's wage, and each peasant still has 25 in hand, so nobody can claim to be in debt. The Summer Palace is the key to the Wall and the Canal, and I want both started before the population card turns. I'm watching Rich: his flattery is too smooth, and he's the one most likely to gather two friends for a revolt.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 50% on the first harvest: 30 each from Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy, 120 in all; Rob refuses his. The crown will build the Summer Palace, all 20 from the reserve, and pay Clark 5 as the Official at the year's end.", map_hl=[1])
say("Clark", "Thirty from each of us, a hundred and twenty to the crown, counted twice and written down once. Majesty, it's in the ledger: fifty percent in the first year, and the Summer Palace before a single stone of the Wall. And Rob, I've noted your refusal too, with the date. A warlord the crown can't tax is a warlord the crown can't control, and that's a page in my book now.",
 "Clark: I pay my thirty like a good servant, and the books stay spotless, so nobody can say I cheated. But a half-share tax in year one and a Warlord who won't pay are two causes for revolt waiting to come true. I'm keeping a note of who nods along when I read them out.")
taxes(.50, skip=(8,), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Summer Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Summer Palace", at="G20", text="Public works: Reina builds the Summer Palace on G20, at the crown's cost of 20.", hover="Reina pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. The Summer Palace comes before the Great Wall and the Province 1 canal.")
say("Clark", "The Customs House starts at three, friends. It's an honest house and it keeps honest books. As for the rest, I'll bid like a clerk, small and steady. Rich, you already own the land under us all, so you don't need the jar and the bowl as well. I'll be writing down who bids against whom.",
 "Clark: The crown took half my grain, so I'm buying cheap pieces and keeping my powder dry, but I'll pay extra to keep a Jar or a Bowl out of the landlord's hands. A Gentry with a full set is a Gentry on the throne, and I'd sooner see the chair empty.")
say("Rich", "Majesty, a palace first. Very wise, a crown ought to have a roof before it has a wall. My rent's in, Silke, Paddy, thank you both, and the Harvest Pile is safe with me. As for tonight's trinkets, I'll bid like a gentleman: modestly, and I'll pay on the nail. And my deeds go no lower than three. Land is land.",
 "Rich: Everyone at this table is skint except the crown and the man in the tent. So I bid small and spread it over three lots, and keep enough back for the twenty grain the set needs. If I come out with one piece I'm the man the others have to deal with.")
say("Silke", "Let's count it out loud, shall we? Thirty to the crown, five to Rich, and the crown sits on a hundred and sixty while I sit on twenty-five. So I'll bid like a woman with twenty-five, and anyone who wants to run these prices up is just feeding the Pile. My Dragon Jar starts at three, and it's worth every grain.",
 "Silke: I want one of each piece cheap, and I'm not getting into a war over any of them, because three quarters of a war goes into the Pile. The only one I'll overpay for is the Bowl, and only if it keeps Rich from finishing a set off my rent.")
say("Paddy", "Well, I paid my thirty and my five and I've twenty-five left to my name, so I won't be running anyone's prices up tonight. I'll put in a fair bid on each piece and no more. And my Bowl goes up at two. It's a plain thing, and I'd rather it went to a good table than sat in my kitchen.",
 "Paddy: I need all three pieces and I can't afford a fight over any of them, so it's six apiece. If I win them all I've still got seven grain to my name and I owe nobody. If someone wants a piece badly enough to pay over six, they can have it, and three quarters of their grain goes to the Pile.")
say("Rob", "Go on, bid, all of you. Buy your pretty jars and bowls and scrolls and put them out on the board where I can see them. I don't need to own a thing, friends. I only need to know where you've put it. And Clark, write this in your book too: the Warlord kept his sixty, and the crown spent twenty on a summer house nine rounds from my tent.",
 "Rob: Every piece they buy tonight ends up on a square, and every square on the board is somewhere I can get to. I'm letting them pay full price for my targets while I keep my grain dry and walk the boys toward that palace.")
act("The auctions, in province order. Rob passes every lot.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
DS = "Take it, then. I can see who's counting and who's just spending."
DP = "Go on then, it's yours. I'll not go hungry over a jar."
DR = "Take it, take it, with my blessing. I'll know where to find it."
lot(2, "CH", [B(5,6), B(5,None,DS), B(6,6), B(6,None,DP), B(3,7)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 3. Silke and Paddy stopped at 6; Rich's limit was 7. Rich puts the Customs House on L10.", at="L10")
lot(3, "LD", [B(5,5), B(5,None,DS), B(6,6), B(6,None,DP), B(2,6)],
 hover="Rich's minimum was 3. Silke stopped at 5. Paddy and Clark both went to 6, and a tie goes by seat order, to Clark. Clark puts the Land Deed Scroll on J14.", at="J14")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(3,6), B(3,None,DR), B(6,6), B(6,None,DP), B(2,7)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 3. Rich and Paddy stopped at 6; Clark's limit was 6, or 9 to keep a piece from Rich. Clark puts the Dragon Jar on J16.", at="J16")
lot(6, "WB", [B(3,6), B(3,None,DR), B(5,6), B(5,None,DS), B(2,7)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. Rich and Silke stopped at 6; Clark's limit was 6, or 9 to keep a piece from Rich. Clark puts the Wooden Bowl on I15, and holds a complete set.", at="I15")
say("Clark", "A scroll, a jar and a bowl, all in the Official's keeping and all paid for on the nail. Rob, since you asked so nicely, they're at the back of my province, behind the clerks, the granary and a great deal of paperwork. And Rich, the Customs House is yours. Try to keep honest books in it.",
 "Clark: That's a full set in year one, but I'm down to about ten grain and I need twenty in hand to use it, so the clever move now is to sit tight and look harmless. They're all in the southern corner, out of Rob's reach for two rounds, and by the time anyone counts my pieces I mean to have the grain to cash them in.")
say("Rich", "A Customs House! Thank you, Clark. Seven grain, and a quarter of it back in your pocket, so we're practically partners. I'll keep it well back from the Wall. No need to tempt our friend in the tent. And Clark, a full set in your first year. Congratulations. I'm sure you'll be writing down who you mean to replace with it.",
 "Rich: Clark has a whole set and only thirty grain, and the set needs twenty of that, so the Official is one good harvest from coming for somebody's chair. I've put my house deep in the south of my province where Rob can't reach it, and from tonight I'm counting who would stand with the crown if Clark moves.")
march({"W1":"H12", "W2":"P25"}, "Rob marches: W1 crosses the north river onto H12 in Clark's Province 2, paying Clark a 5 toll and raiding 5 from him; W2 comes ashore on P25 in Silke's Province 5, raiding 5 from her. He hires no one.")
say("Rob", "Clark, you can stack paperwork as high as the Wall and I'll still read it. Here's five for your river toll, and I'm taking five back for the walk. We'll call that our first transaction. Silke, sorry, love, the sea air makes me peckish. That's five off the coast, and nobody in this province ordered it except me.",
 "Rob: W1 crosses the river into Clark's country and the Land Deed's two steps off now, with the bowl and jar right behind it. W2 lands on the coast and starts the long walk inland toward that brand-new Summer Palace.")
salaries({2:5}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 5 as the Official.")
# ============================== YEAR 2 ==============================
R(2, "Spring Floods", "rainflood")
act("Reina, the Emperor, reads the I Ching herself: Spring Floods. Provinces 1, 3 and 6 have a good harvest; Provinces 2 and 5 and the Warlord's desert a bad one. The population stays at 100 million, x1.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Reina", "\"Spring Floods. Heaven waters the land generously. In some places, far too generously.\" Provinces 1, 3, 6 and 7 have a good harvest. Provinces 2, 4, 5 and 8 have a bad one. The population holds at 100 million, times one, so read your first row. And yes, Heaven was kind to the crown's own province. Clark, Silke, I'm sorry about the water, but the empire still has to be built.",
 "Reina: Good fields for me means the crown can tax hard again this year and keep the Pagoda on schedule. Silke and Clark both got flooded, though, and a flooded peasant starts counting her grievances, so I'll watch which of them looks at Rich first.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "The pile pays out, friends, and gladly: Your Majesty forty, Clark ten, Silke ten, Paddy sixty, and sixty to your humble servant, since the floods were so kind to Province 3. Rent stays at the customary five each. I won't squeeze a flooded field, Silke, a landlord's only as good as his tenants' goodwill. I do hope the crown remembers the flooded provinces when the tax comes round, Your Majesty. Heaven has already taken its share there.",
 "Rich: Ninety-odd grain in my granary and two peasants who'll remember I didn't raise the rent in a bad year. Funny how the crown's own province never seems to flood; I'm keeping count of who's paying this tax and who's getting tired of it.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "Fifty percent again, the same rate for every province, flooded or not. Heaven takes its share, Rich, and the crown takes its own. The Winter Palace goes up this year, and it opens the way for the southern canals. Clark, your five, as before.",
 "Reina: Rich asked me in front of the whole table to go easy on the flooded provinces, and a crown that gives way on the first grumble will be grumbled at forever. I'm keeping the rate and building the palace, and I'll watch whether Silke and Clark start whispering with him.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 50% again: Clark owes 5, Rich 30, Silke 5 and Paddy 30, 70 in all; Rob's income is nil. The crown will build the Winter Palace, all 20 from the reserve, and pay Clark 5 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1])
act("Storyteller's tip, to Silke, said to the whole table: a revolt can be called at any step, by anyone, the Warlord included (Section 16). It needs its caller and two others agreeing on a cause, and the board card lists the causes. Once it's called with two agreeing, play stops until it stands or is dropped. The Emperor may answer it, and it stands only if the caller and two others still back it. One called now, before the tax is paid, voids this year's tax. Under Chaos nobody pays tax, salaries or works, and nobody can buy new land. The harvest, the rent, the auctions and the Warlord carry on. The reserve is sealed: nobody spends it, and it passes whole to whoever is acclaimed. The throne then goes only by acclamation. Anyone but the deposed Emperor may claim it, a peasant or the Warlord included, and the claimants are counted on grain in hand, soldiers on the board and friends.", map_hl=[5])
act("Before Clark collects, every seat that owes the tax, and Rob, answers at the same moment: pay it, or call or back a revolt.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6,8])
say("Clark", "Fifty percent in Year 1, Your Majesty, and fifty percent again on a flooded field. Two years running is a pattern, and patterns are what I write down. I'll collect if I'm asked to collect. But if anyone at this table names excessive tax as a cause, they'll find my name beside theirs, and I'll bring the ledger to prove it.",
 "Clark: Page two of my grievances: same rate on drowned land as dry, two years straight, and that's one cause for revolt on the books. I could buy my way into that chair tonight, but a throne you buy has to be guarded, and a throne the table hands you comes with friends. So I'll back the complaint, keep my set in the drawer, and watch who else puts their hand up.")
say('Rich', "Your Majesty, I've praised this crown every year and I meant it. But fifty in the hundred, two years running, on drowned fields the same as dry ones? That isn't Heaven's share. If anyone at this table says the tax is too heavy, they'll have Richard Baron's name beside theirs. And once I give my name, I don't take it back.",
 "Rich: I'm not going to call it, because the man who calls a revolt is the first one the crown remembers. But I've said in front of everyone that I'll back it, and I keep my word to the letter, so now every one of them knows exactly where to find me.")
say('Silke', "Fifty percent last year, fifty percent this year, and fifty percent of a flooded field. I paid thirty last spring and got a palace I'll never see the inside of. Your Majesty, that's not a tax, that's a habit. I'm calling it: the taxes are consistently excessive. Clark, your province drowned too. Paddy, you're down thirty for a Winter Palace. Who's with me?",
 'Silke: Calling it now, before Clark collects, voids this year\'s tax, and the reserve stays sealed for whoever comes next. Rich wants the crown "remembering the flooded provinces", so we\'ll see whether he really means that or just likes how it sounds.')
say('Paddy', "Half, Your Majesty, two years running, and the same half from a drowned field as from a dry one. I'm not one for shouting, and I've paid my thirty before, but if someone stands up and calls the tax excessive, Paddy Doyle will stand with them. Whoever sits on that throne after, they'll have to show me rice in Province 6 before I cheer.",
 "Paddy: I won't light the fire myself, I've no stomach for being the one who started it. But fifty percent every year, flood or no flood, is a habit now, not a hardship, and if two others say so I'll make the third.")
say('Rob', "Fifty percent again, on a flooded field? Your Majesty, even I don't take half of a drowned man's rice, and I'm the villain of this table. Clark, Silke, you're the ones paying into a palace with water up to your ankles. Rich, Paddy, you heard her: same rate next year, and the year after. I call it. Taxes are consistently excessive. Who's with me? Put your hands up and keep your grain.",
 "Rob: No tax, no salaries, and no crown to tell me where I'm allowed to raid. If two of them say yes, the Emperor's seat is open and I'm the only one at the table with an army.")
revolt_called("Silke and Rob together call a revolt: the taxes are consistently excessive. Clark, Rich and Paddy all agree the cause exists. Play stops until the revolt stands or is dropped, and the Emperor may answer it.")
say("Reina", "You want a smaller crown? Then look at what you're tearing down. Fifty percent built a palace in one year, and it will build your canals after the next. I won't haggle with a mob over Heaven's own share. Silke, Paddy, the canals are coming to you. Rich, Clark, you'll remember whose table fed you. Back me, or live with the Chaos you asked for.",
 "Reina: Cutting the tax now would tell them that one loud spring can bend the crown, and then the bending would never stop. I'm betting they're more afraid of Chaos than of me, and if I've got that wrong, the reserve stays sealed and I'll still have a say in who sits on the throne next.")
act("Reina answers the revolt: no cut, the tax stays at 50%, and a promise that the Grand Canals in Provinces 5 and 6 come next once the palaces stand. She pays nothing.", map_hl=[1])
act("The callers and backers answer at the same moment: stand by the revolt, or take it back.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6,8])
say("Clark", "Your Majesty, a promise of canals after the palaces is a promise written on water, and this year we've had more than enough of that. You were asked to cut it and you said 'Heaven's share' again, so that answer goes in the ledger too. My name stays where I put it. And Rich, I'll be watching closely to see who comes looking for friends once the chair is empty.",
 "Clark: She had one chance to save it and she doubled down, so the revolt stands and my set can't touch the throne now. I didn't want it that way anyway. From here the crown goes to whoever the table trusts, and I've spent two years writing down everyone's grievances for them, so I mean that to be me and not the landlord.")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, your table did feed me, and I've thanked you for it every year. But a promise of canals after the palaces isn't a cut, it's the same fifty with a ribbon tied on it. I said my name stays where I put it, and it stays. And to all of you, since the throne goes by acclamation now: my granary's full, my door's open, and I'm very easy to find.",
 "Rich: Over a hundred grain in hand and no tax to pay this year. Every claimant at this table is going to need the Gentry's friendship, and I plan to make them ask for it nicely.")
say("Silke", 'Canals "once the palaces stand", Your Majesty. That\'s two palaces of my grain before I see one ditch of water, and my field\'s already got plenty of that. A promise with no cut in it is just the same tax with a nicer speech on top. I stand. And Rich, you said you don\'t take your name back. Everyone heard you.',
 "Silke: The crown answered with a promise and not one grain off, so my name stays where it is, and I've just held Rich to his word in front of the whole table. Once there's no throne, a flooded peasant with twenty-six grain in hand suddenly counts for something, and I mean to make every claimant bid for me.")
say("Paddy", 'Your Majesty, a canal promised "once the palaces stand" is a canal after your palaces, paid for out of my rice in the meantime. I\'d take one bowl in my hand over a canal in a speech. My name stays where I put it.',
 "Paddy: She had the chance to cut the tax by even a little, and she chose to threaten us instead. A promise with no grain behind it doesn't feed Province 6, so I'm standing, and now I'll have to be very careful who I cheer for next.")
say("Rob", "Your Majesty offered us canals later and fifty percent now. I've made better promises with a sword at a toll gate. My name stays, and I'll tell you all now: when that seat's empty, I'm putting myself forward for it, and I won't be bought off cheap.",
 "Rob: Chaos means no crown to send soldiers after me and nobody who can tell me where I'm allowed to raid. I've got two armies on the board and sixty-five in grain, so let's see who'd dare count against me.")
revolt("All five stand by the revolt: Silke, Rob, Clark, Rich and Paddy. The revolt stands and Chaos begins. This year's tax is void, the Winter Palace isn't built and no salaries are paid. The reserve, 195, is sealed in Province 1 and passes whole to whoever is acclaimed. Reina is deposed: she stays in Province 1, can't claim the throne, and may back a claimant.")
act("The first acclamation, in seat order: each player claims the throne, backs a claimant or stands aside. Reina, deposed, may only back or stand aside.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Reina", "So all five of you stood, and not one of you thought to stand for the chair you emptied. Well, it's empty now. When someone has the nerve to claim it, I'll hear what they mean to build. Rich, your door's open, is it? I remember a man who flattered this throne every year he ate at its table. Rob, a toll-gate bandit as Emperor? You'll have to do better than that.",
 "Reina: Nobody has claimed yet, and I'm not going to give my name away before I know who's asking for it. The one person who'll never get it is the flatterer who turned his coat the moment the wind changed.")
say("Clark", "Since nobody else has the nerve, Your Majesty, I'll be the first. I've collected this crown's tax and I've kept its books, so I know exactly what that chair costs and who it's cost. My terms are a fair rate, a lighter one on flooded fields, and works that actually get built. Rich, I'm sure your door's very open, but I've never yet met a landlord who opened his door without charging for it.",
 "Clark: That set in my drawer could have bought me the throne in any other year, and I'm glad I never spent it on that, because a throne the table gives is the only one worth having. Rich has more grain than I do and Rob has the soldiers, so friends are where I can win, and I've been keeping notes on everyone's grievances for two years now.")
say("Rich", "Clark, you're quite right that I've never opened my door for free, and you've never opened your ledger without meaning to use it. I won't watch this empire handed over on twenty-six grain. So I'm putting my granary into the count, a hundred and four and a half of it. My terms are the customary five in rent, a light tax, and a full Harvest Pile for every province. And anyone who'd like my granary behind them instead of in front of them knows where I live.",
 "Rich: I never wanted the chair. But Clark with the crown and his ledger is the one outcome I can't stand, and my grain is the biggest count at this table. Now he has to get past me, and so does everyone else.")
say("Silke", "A hundred and four and a half, Rich, and I can count every grain of it, because about forty of them came out of my field and Paddy's as rent and pile shares. You don't get to buy the chair with that. Clark, you said a lighter rate on flooded fields. Province 5 floods. Say it in front of everyone and my name's beside yours.",
 "Silke: Rich leads on grain, and now Clark leads on friends, so neither of them leads alone and the chair stays empty while they bid against each other. My landlord isn't getting the throne with my rent, and Clark will have to keep that flooded-field promise to hold on to me.")
act("Storyteller's note, to the table: Rich has had 20 in rent from Silke and Paddy over the two years (5 each a year), and no auction shares, since he has sold nothing. The rest of his 104.5 is his harvests.")
say("Paddy", "Clark, you've a lighter rate for flooded fields, and Rich, you've a full pile, but I haven't heard either of you say a single word about Province 6, or tell me which work gets built first and when. I'm not cheering for a ledger or a granary. When one of you can tell me what gets built and where, I'll listen.",
 "Paddy: We all stood up to empty that chair, so I won't fill it in a hurry with the first name called. I've never liked the way Clark counts, and Rich's grain came partly out of my field, so they'll both have to earn me.")
say("Rob", "A ledger, a granary, and not one sword between them. Reina, you said a toll-gate bandit would have to do better, so here's better: I claim the throne, with two soldiers on the board who are the only reason anyone's harvest is still standing. My terms are simple. Back me and your squares are safe from me. Don't, and I'll come and collect the tax myself, one raid at a time, starting with that Deed of yours, Clark, which is two rounds from my W1.",
 "Rob: Rich has the grain, Clark has a friend, and I've got the only soldiers at this table, so that's three counts with three different leaders and no Emperor. That suits me fine, because while they argue my army keeps marching.")
acclamation({2:[5], 3:[], 8:[]}, "The count: Clark claims, backed by Silke; Rich claims; Rob claims. Reina and Paddy stand aside. Rich leads on grain (104.5), Rob on soldiers (2) and Clark on friends (1). Each leads one count, so nobody is acclaimed, and Chaos goes on.")
say("Clark", "Since Silke wants it said in front of everyone, here it is: under my crown, any flooded province pays half the rate that year, and that goes in the ledger as a promise kept, not a speech. Paddy, the first canal goes to Province 6, then Province 5, and I'll have the year written beside each one before I'm seated. As for the auctions, I've got my set, so I'll only bid if somebody's trying to buy himself a chair he can't be given.",
 "Clark: My set's already complete, so this year every grain I hold is a vote in the count, and I'm not spending it on pottery. If Rich wants a Jar or a Bowl, though, I'll make him pay into the pile for it, so his granary looks a little less like a crown.")
say("Rich", "Silke, every grain of rent was the customary five, and the Storyteller's just confirmed it, so I'll thank you not to make me a robber in front of company. My Deed goes up at three, as before, because land is land. As for the Jar and the Bowl, I'll bid fairly and no higher. I won't drain the pile in a war over crockery while the chair sits empty.",
 "Rich: My granary is the count I lead on, so I'm not going to spend it down on pottery. Ten apiece and not a grain more. If the pieces come cheap, I'm a step nearer my set; if not, I'm still the richest claimant at the table.")
say("Silke", "Thank you, Storyteller: twenty in rent, then, and the rest is harvest. That's still twenty of mine and Paddy's sitting in that granary. I'm bidding small, because no Emperor means no tax, and I'll keep my grain, thanks. Paddy, we both need the House and the Deed. I'm not going to fight you up to thirty so that three quarters of it lands back in Rich's pile.",
 "Silke: Every big bid hands three quarters to the Harvest Pile Rich runs, so I'm only bidding cheap. I'll only go higher to keep the Bowl from Rich. That's the one lot where he'd be finishing his set off my landlord's door.")
say("Paddy", "I lost the Deed last year on a tie at six, so this year I'll go a little further, but only a little. I'm not burning my winter grain in a bidding war while there's nobody on the throne to build anything with it. My bowl goes up at two, same as always.",
 "Paddy: With no Emperor, every grain I keep counts in the next tally, so I'll pay fair for the pieces I need and let the rest go. Silke's short of grain and Rich has plenty, so the Jar is the one I'll most likely lose.")
say("Rob", "Same as always: buy whatever you like, and put it wherever you like. Just remember I'll be taking a good look at where it ends up. And Rich, every grain you bid is one fewer in that famous granary of yours, so do go ahead and spend.",
 "Rob: My grain stays in my pocket, since it's counted when they pick an Emperor. Meanwhile every piece they buy is one more thing on the board for me to come and take.")
act("The auctions, in province order. Rob passes every lot.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Rich", "I said ten and no higher, Paddy, and I meant it. If you want the Jar badly enough to go past that, it's yours. If you don't, then the seat order is mine, fair and square.",
 "Rich: I made that promise in front of the whole table, and I keep promises to the letter. If Paddy's too careful to raise, the Jar comes to me at ten and my word still holds.")
say("Paddy", "I lost one piece on a seat-order tie last year, Rich, and I'd rather not lose two that way. Thirteen, and that's my last word on a jar.",
 "Paddy: That Jar finishes my pieces, and that's worth three more grain to me, though not a war. If Rich wants it more than that, he can have it, and most of what he pays goes back into the pile anyway.")
DS2 = "Fine. Take it, and I'll keep my grain for when the chair's worth bidding for."
DP2 = "That's past what it's worth to me. Take it, and good luck to you."
DR2 = "Take it, with my blessing. My granary stays full for the count."
DC2 = "Take it, then. I've written down what you paid."
lot(2, "CH", [B(5,4), B(5,None,DS2), B(6,5)], hover="Clark's minimum was 2. Silke stopped at 4; Paddy's limit was 12. Paddy puts the Customs House on S23.", at="S23")
lot(3, "LD", [B(5,4), B(5,None,DS2), B(6,5)], hover="Rich's minimum was 3. Silke stopped at 4; Paddy's limit was 12. Paddy puts the Land Deed Scroll on S24.", at="S24")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(3,10), B(3,None,DR2), B(6,11)], hover="Silke's minimum was 3. Rich and Paddy both reached 10, a tie Rich would win by seat order; in one live raise Rich held at 10 and Paddy went to 13. Paddy puts the Dragon Jar on S25, and holds a complete set.", at="S25")
lot(6, "WB", [B(2,4), B(2,None,DC2), B(5,7), B(5,None,DS2), B(3,8)], hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. Clark would go to 4 only to keep it from Rich; Silke to 7 to keep it from Rich; Rich's limit was 10. Rich puts the Wooden Bowl on M12.", at="M12")
say("Paddy", "Well, that's the House, the Deed and the Jar all in Province 6, and I didn't expect to be saying that tonight. They're going right down by the sea, as far from any sword as my land runs. Rob, you're welcome to look, from a distance.",
 "Paddy: Three pieces for twenty-one grain is a fair spring's work, and I've tucked them into the far corner where the Warlord can't reach them for a good while. A full set's no good for the throne under Chaos, but it's leverage, and now Clark and Rich both have a reason to court me properly.")
say("Rich", "A handsome bowl, Paddy, and a fair price. It goes deep in Province 3, well away from any toll gates. Rob, I'm sure you understand. And congratulations on your full set, Paddy. You'll find the Gentry is a very good friend to a man with a full set and no chair to take it to.",
 "Rich: Two pieces of three, and the Bowl is tucked away where neither of Rob's soldiers can reach it. Paddy has just become the most interesting man at this table, because a full set under Chaos still unseats anybody except the Emperor, and I'd like him grateful to me before he decides who to use it on.")
march({"W1":"I13", "W2":"Q24"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto I13 in Clark's Province 2, raiding 5 from him; W2 comes ashore on Q24 in Paddy's Province 6, raiding 5 from him. He hires no one.")
say("Rob", "Paddy, 'from a distance' is three rounds, and I've just made it two. Clark, that's five from Province 2 and your Deed is one step away, so write it in the ledger as the Warlord's tax. With nobody on the throne, I'm the only one at this table still collecting.",
 "Rob: W1 is heading for Clark's Deed and W2 is going down the coast after Paddy's brand-new set. Ten grain in my pocket this round, and every candidate for that chair has to watch it happen.")
act("No salaries are paid under Chaos. Year 2 ends with no Emperor: Clark, Rich and Rob have claimed, Silke backs Clark, and Reina and Paddy stand aside.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
# ===== CHAPTER 2: Swords Eat
# ============================== YEAR 3 ==============================
R(3, "Careless Prosperity", "careless")
act("Reina, deposed, reads the I Ching herself, unpaid: Careless Prosperity. Every province has a good harvest, and every player plays a personal disaster. The population stays at 100 million, x1.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Reina", 'Careless Prosperity. "Good times make careless people." Every province brings in a good harvest, and every one of us plays a Personal Disaster, so each of you turn the next page in your booklet. The population holds at 100 million, times one, so read the x1 row. Full granaries and no crown to tax them. Enjoy it while it lasts, and remember Heaven just called you careless.',
 "Reina: A fat year with nobody to tax it makes Chaos look cheap, so none of the claimants will pay much for my backing yet, and I won't give it to them cheap either. Let them get careless. The bill comes due the first time a harvest fails with no crown to buy land or raise a wall.")
disaster(1, "Reina pays 4, out of the sealed treasury.")
disaster(2, "Clark pays 4.")
disaster(3, "Rich pays 5.")
disaster(5, "Silke pays 4.")
disaster(6, "Paddy pays 4.")
disaster(8, "Rob may raid nothing in Provinces 5, 6 and 7 this year.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "A golden harvest, friends, sixty to nearly every house, paid out of the Pile in full and on time, as always. And the rent stays at the customary five. Heaven calls us careless, but nobody will ever say the Gentry was greedy in a fat year. Paddy, Silke, I'd call that good neighbours. I'd remember it, too, if I were counting friends.",
 "Rich: Five on a good harvest costs me nothing and buys me goodwill I can call in when the fields go bad. Meanwhile I'm sitting on a hundred and fifty-two, the biggest granary of any claimant, so whoever wants the chair will have to come and see me first.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("Under Chaos there is no tax and no public works. Every piece has been sold out of stock and nobody has sold one back, so there are no auctions this year.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6])
march({"W1":"J14", "W2":"R25"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto J14 in Clark's Province 2 and raids the Land Deed Scroll standing there, carrying it off to M15 in the desert; W2 walks along the sea to R25, off Paddy's coast, and raids nothing.", take={"W1":("LD","M15")})
hire_soldier("M16", "Rob hires a third soldier, W3, for 20, paid to the Harvest Pile, on M16 in the desert.")
say("Rob", "Careless Prosperity, Heaven says. Well, Clark, you got careless with your Deed, so it's coming out to the desert for a nice long rest at M15. And Paddy, Heaven's tied my hands on your coast this year, so I'm only strolling down to R25 to admire the view. It'll still be there next year. So will I. Oh, and I've hired a third lad. Big harvests make for cheap swords. Next time you count for the chair, count him too.",
 "Rob: First piece of the set goes in the bag: Clark's Deed is mine, W2 sits two steps off Paddy's whole row waiting for the ban to lift, and a third soldier means three swords on the board for the next count. Rich can buy his friends with fair rent; I'm buying mine with fear.")
act("The year ends with the claims put to the table again. Nobody changes: Clark claims, backed by Silke; Rich and Rob claim; Reina and Paddy stand aside. Rich leads on grain (162.5), Rob on soldiers (3) and Clark on friends (1), and Chaos goes on.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Reina", "Rich, you pay out of the Pile in a fat year and call it generosity. Rob, you steal a deed and call it a claim. Clark, you still haven't put a single canal in writing. My name is the only thing in this province that isn't sealed, and none of you has earned it yet. I'll sit here and watch you carry on.",
 'Reina: I keep standing aside, because backing Clark now only gets him a second friend: Rich still leads on grain, Rob on soldiers, and the count stays split three ways. While the granaries are full nobody needs a crown, so my backing goes to whoever wants it badly enough to pay for it in walls and canals.')
say("Clark", "I stand where I stood, and Silke stands with me. One friend is still one more than the granary has. Rob, the Deed's in the ledger: taken from J14, carried to M15, Year 3, in a year Heaven said was fat. A man who takes in a good year will take more in a bad one, and I'll remind the table of that when the harvests turn. Rich, five in rent in a sixty-grain year isn't kindness, it's the least you could get away with. Paddy, my word on that canal still stands, and so does my claim.",
 "Clark: Rob broke my set, but under Chaos a set can't reach the chair anyway, so all he's really done is give me another grievance to write down. Rich has the grain and Rob has the swords. I've got the only thing that lasts past a lean year, which is people who'd rather I counted the tax than either of them.")
say("Rich", 'My claim stands, and so does my granary: a hundred and sixty-two and a half, counted honestly and free for anyone to inspect. Rob, three swords is a fine thing to look at, but swords eat, and every one of them was paid for out of a harvest my Pile handed you. Clark, one friend is a start. Do keep writing it down.',
 "Rich: Three claimants each leading one count means nobody gets the chair, and no chair means no tax on my harvest, so I'm happy to sit on the biggest grain pile at the table and wait. Whoever finally wants to be crowned will have to buy my backing, and I won't sell it cheap.")
say("Silke", "Customary five, Rich? It's the same five you took off me last year, it's just politer this time. The Pile paid out sixty because Heaven made it rain, not because you were being generous. You're sitting on a hundred and sixty-two and a half, I'm on eighty, and that's the only count I care about. My name stays on Clark's claim. Clark, Rob just walked off with your Deed and you're down to seventy-eight and a half, so the half rate on flooded fields is the least of what you owe me now. Think about what comes next.",
 "Silke: No crown means no tax, so this is the first fat year I've had and I'm not going to rush it. Clark keeps my backing because it keeps Rich out of the chair, but every year he holds it he has to offer me a bit more.")
say("Paddy", "Thank you for the five, Rich, honestly. But a fair rent in a fat year isn't a canal, and I've still not heard one of you say a spade's going into Province 6. Rob, I'll count your third lad when he's digging a ditch. Until then my name stays in my pocket.",
 "Paddy: Every claimant's been telling me what they won't take off me, and not one has said what they'll build. I'll stand aside and keep my grain dry. Rob's soldier is sitting one square from my set, so next year I'll be looking hard at who'd actually stop him.")
say("Rob", "My claim stands, on three good swords and nothing else. Rich has the grain, Clark has a friend, and I have the only men at this table who can walk into your fields. Count it again next year if you like. Until somebody meets my price, nobody sits in that chair, and I'm in no hurry at all.",
 "Rob: Three ways deadlocked suits me fine: no Emperor means nobody to pay me off and nobody to send men after me, so the raiding just goes on. Paddy's row is one step from W2, and the ban on his coast lifts next year.")
# ============================== YEAR 4 ==============================
R(4, "Imperial Charity", "chest")
act("Reina, deposed, reads the I Ching herself, unpaid: Imperial Charity. Every province has an average harvest, and the population stays at 100 million, x1.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Reina", 'Imperial Charity. "Moved by the suffering of his people, the Emperor opens his purse. Slightly." Every province brings in an average harvest, and each of you gets 2 from the reserve: Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob, 10 in all. The population holds at 100 million, times one, so read the x1 row. So the treasury you sealed is paying you now, even with nobody in the chair. Remember whose it was the next time you count.',
 "Reina: That makes ten out of my sealed treasury, handed to the five people who emptied my chair, and they'll still call the crown useless. An average harvest is less padding than a fat one, so let's see how long Rob's three swords and Rich's granary can keep them all comfortable.")
act("The card's charity comes out of the sealed treasury in Province 1, since there is no Emperor: 2 each to Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob, 10 in all.", deltas={pid(1):-10, pid(2):2, pid(3):2, pid(5):2, pid(6):2, pid(8):2}, map_hl=[1])
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "Thirty-five to every peasant house, paid out to the grain, and the rent stays at five. Silke, if it's the same five as last year, then you can at least say I'm a man who keeps to his word. Paddy, no spade goes in anywhere until somebody's in that chair, and you can't dig a canal with a promise, not even one of Clark's.",
 'Rich: Five again, on an average year, so nobody at this table can say the Gentry squeezed them while the crown was gone. The canal Paddy keeps asking for is the one thing every claimant has to offer him, and whoever puts the grain behind it will need my granary to do it.')
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("Under Chaos there is no tax and no public works, and with every piece already sold out of stock there are no auctions.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6])
march({"W1":"K13", "W2":"S24", "W3":"L15"}, "Rob marches: W1 onto K13 and W3 onto L15, both in Rich's Province 3, raiding 5 from him each; W2 steps onto S24 in Paddy's Province 6 and carries off the Land Deed Scroll standing there to T24 in the desert.", take={"W2":("LD","T24")})
say("Rob", "Paddy, you wanted to see my third lad digging. Well, he's not, but his mate's just dug your Land Deed out of S24 and it's off to T24 for some sea air. Rich, you said swords eat. They do, and tonight they're eating out of Province 3, two of them, five apiece. Thank the Pile for me.",
 "Rob: That's Paddy's piece in the bag, two of four, and both W1 and W3 are now walking into Rich's country with his Customs House and his Bowl in their sights. The richest man at the table gets to find out what a claim on grain is worth when the grain keeps walking off.")
say("Reina", "That's two broken sets and two raids on the Gentry's own fields, all in a year Heaven called average. Rich, it looks like your granary can't keep a sword off your own doorstep. Clark, I'm still waiting on that writing. My name goes to whoever gives me walls over the north and spades in the coast, on paper and in that order. Until then I stay where I am.",
 "Reina: The cost is starting to show, with Rob picking off sets and taking grain out of Province 3, but all three of them can still afford to wait, so my backing stays in my pocket. When one of them finally comes to me with the works written down, I'll know they've started feeling it.")
say("Clark", "Reina wants it in writing, so here it is, read into the record: in the first year I sit, the first canal goes to Province 6, the second to Province 5 the year after, and any flooded province pays half the rate in the year it floods. Paddy, Rob has now taken your Deed the same way he took mine, from the same desert, so there are two pages in my ledger with his name on them. Rich, the Pile's been raided twice in one night, and the only crown that'll ever protect a granary is one the table actually agrees on.",
 "Clark: Rob is raiding all of us, so the only fix is a crown with friends behind it, and I've just put the canal in writing so that Paddy's spare name has somewhere to go. I don't need him tonight. I need him when the table gets tired of feeding Rob's soldiers.")
say("Rich", "Ten grain, Rob, out of a hundred and ninety-nine and a half. Your lads ate well, and my claim is still sitting here bigger than yours and Clark's put together. Paddy, you've lost your Deed to the same swords that dined in my province tonight, so I'd say the two of us have something to talk about.",
 "Rich: Rob's raids cost me ten and cost Paddy his set, which leaves Paddy holding a Dragon Jar that's no use to him on its own, and that's the one piece I still need. Chaos goes on, my granary is still the biggest at the table, and now I've got something to buy.")
say("Silke", "Fine, Rich, you keep your word, and your word is five. I've said it. Now let's count. You're on a hundred and ninety-nine and a half, Clark's on a hundred and fifteen and a half, Rob's on ninety-seven, and I'm on a hundred and twelve with nothing taken off me by the crown for two years running. That's the best deal this coast has had since I sat down. Clark, my name's still on your claim, but all you've given me so far is one promise about flooded fields. If you want me there when the harvests turn, I want it written down before then, not after.",
 "Silke: Two Deeds have gone into the desert and not one grain has gone to the crown. Chaos is costing Clark and Paddy, but it isn't costing me yet. Clark keeps my name because Rich can't have it, and I'll let him wait a little longer for anything more.")
say("Paddy", "Rob, you've a soldier standing in my field and my Deed's out in the sand at T24, and I'll not forget it. Rich, you're right that a promise doesn't dig a canal, but neither does a full granary. Whichever of you tells me plainly how you'd get that soldier off my coast and a spade into Province 6 gets my name. Nobody has yet.",
 "Paddy: Losing the Deed hurts. Backing the first claimant who's kind to me would only crown someone who's never said a word about Rob's swords, so I'm holding my name back and keeping the grain I've got.")
say("Rob", "Still claiming, still three swords, still nobody's friend, and proud of it. Rich, you're a hundred and ninety-nine and a half and climbing, and every grain of it is a good reason for my lads to come and visit. Paddy, Clark, two broken sets and not one Emperor to complain to. Funny how that works.",
 'Rob: Two pieces down out of four, Rich and Silke still to go, and the count is as stuck as ever, which is just how I want it. As long as nobody sits in that chair, nobody can buy me off or send men after me.')
say("Paddy", "Clark, that's the first time anyone's put Province 6 on paper, and I heard it. I'm still standing aside, mind. Rich, you're right that we've both had Rob's lads in our fields, so if you want to talk, tell me how you'd keep them out. That's the thing I'd care about.",
 "Paddy: Twenty-five grain for a Jar I paid eleven for sounds lovely, until you see it hands Rich a full set that could put him in my own seat. I'll keep the Jar and go and find myself another Deed.")
act("The year ends with the claims put to the table again. Nobody changes: Clark claims, backed by Silke; Rich and Rob claim; Reina and Paddy stand aside. Rich leads on grain (199.5), Rob on soldiers (3) and Clark on friends (1), and Chaos goes on. Paddy turns down a private offer from Rich for his Dragon Jar.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
# ===== CHAPTER 3: A Figure, Not a Speech
# ============================== YEAR 5 ==============================
R(5, "Drought", "blight")
act("Reina, deposed, reads the I Ching herself, unpaid: Drought. Every province has an average harvest, and the population stays at 100 million, x1.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say('Reina', '"Drought. Not a cloud in months. The locusts, at least, are having a fine year. All provinces collect an average harvest." The counter stands at a hundred million, so the multiple is still x1. Read the first row of your tables. That\'s three years now of an average harvest with no tax on it and not one stone laid. When the people double, gentlemen, you\'ll find that land can\'t be bought from an empty chair.',
 "Reina: Heaven has handed them another easy year, and it's the last one they'll get cheap. When the population turns and land opens at twenty a square, every one of them will need a crown to share the cost, and my name goes to whichever of them writes the Wall in next to the canals.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say('Rich', "The harvest's paid, every grain of it, straight from the pile. Thirty-five to each of you good farmers, and the customary five back to me in rent, as I promised and as I'll keep promising, drought or no drought. And Your Majesty, forgive me, Madam, is quite right: when the people double, land will want a buyer and a chair to share the cost. I hear her. I'm sure we all do.",
 "Rich: Five, same as every year. A promise kept is cheaper than a favour bought, and the day someone has to be acclaimed I want both peasants remembering who never squeezed them. Reina's sitting on two hundred and fifty-one and the sealed treasury besides, and she's started talking like someone who'll be the one buying the land, so I'm keeping my claim and keeping an eye on which way the table leans.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("Under Chaos there is no tax and no public works, and with every piece already sold out of stock there are no auctions.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6])
march({"W1":"L12", "W2":"S25", "W3":"M14"}, "Rob marches: W1 onto L12 and W3 onto M14, both in Rich's Province 3, raiding 5 from him each; W2 steps onto S25 in Paddy's Province 6 and carries off the Dragon Jar standing there to T23 in the desert. W1 now stands one step from Rich's Wooden Bowl on M12.", take={"W2":("DJ","T23")})
say('Rob', "A drought, Your Majesty-who-isn't? Then my lads will have to drink something else. Paddy, your Dragon Jar is coming to live in the desert with your Deed, so they can keep each other company. Rich, keep your rent, I'm helping myself to Province 3 instead, five from each of my boys. And when the people double and you all go looking for someone to sit in the chair, remember to count my three swords first.",
 "Rob: W1 is one step from Rich's Wooden Bowl now, so next round it walks into my desert, and Paddy's jar goes today just because I can. That leaves Silke's coast, and she has nothing on the board I can't wait for.")
say('Reina', "Still aside. Rich, a man who pays himself rent out of his own pile hasn't earned my name. Rob, your swords have carried off a jar and built nothing. Clark, your canals are on paper, and I respect that, but I still don't see a single line about the Wall over the north or the Pagoda. Write those in beside the spades and you'll find out what my name is worth. Until then, enjoy your drought.",
 "Reina: The count is a three-way deadlock and my one friend wouldn't break it this year anyway, so I'm keeping my name for the moment it decides something. Clark is the only one close, and I want the Wall in writing before I lift a finger for him.")
say('Clark', "My claim stands, and so do my works. They're in the record already: Province 6's canal in my first year, Province 5's the year after, and half the rate on any drowned field. Her Majesty-that-was is right that a chair is needed when the people double. I'd only add that the chair should go to someone who has written down what he'll build, not to the man with the biggest granary or the most swords. Rob, that's two Deeds and now a Jar in your desert, and I have every square written down. Rich, five from each of Rob's boys out of Province 3 this year. Funny how the landlord's grain buys him no friends.",
 "Clark: I'm holding still, because one honest friend is worth more to me than eighty grain or three soldiers, and once land goes on sale the table will start counting friends in earnest. Rich's Bowl is one step from Rob's W1. If his set goes the way Paddy's did, the landlord has nothing left to bargain with but grain.")
say('Rich', "My claim stands where it stood, gentlemen, on the biggest granary at this table, and I'll say it again, it was never filled from anyone's ruin. Rob, your lads have now eaten twenty of my grain in two years, so don't ask me to count your swords as friends. And Paddy, I'm truly sorry about your Jar. The Gentry remembers who's been robbed as well as who's been kind.",
 "Rich: Nobody leads more than one count, so the chair stays empty another year and my granary keeps growing, which suits me fine for now. But Rob has taken a piece out of Paddy's set and his soldiers are standing one step from my Bowl, so whoever finally gets the crown will need me, and I mean to choose the moment.")
say('Silke', "My name stays on Clark's claim. Let's count it out, though: three years of thirty-five a harvest, and Rich has taken his five out of every one of them. Clark, your canal only counts if somebody's sitting in that chair to dig it, and when the people double I want my coast buying land, not looking at it. So next year your offer has to get bigger, or it stops being an offer.",
 "Silke: I stay with Clark because Rich wants me against him, and the day I go to Rich is the day I've got nothing left to bargain with. But Her Majesty-that-was has a point: when that new land comes up, nobody gets a square without a chair, so Clark needs to start acting like he's going to sit in it.")
say('Paddy', "That's my Jar gone to the desert now, after my Deed, and not one of you three could have stopped it, because there's no chair to stop it from. I'm still standing aside, but I'll tell you straight: I'm nearly done waiting. The first of you who puts a real figure on a canal for Province 6 and a plan to keep Rob's lads off my coast, a figure and not a speech, gets my name next year.",
 "Paddy: Backing anyone tonight wouldn't crown them, since Rich has the grain, Rob has the swords and Clark has the friends, so I'll keep my name back one more year. But Chaos has cost me two pieces of my set now, and I'm not stubborn enough to let it take the third.")
say('Rob', "Still claiming, and still the only man here with anything sharper than a ledger. Rich has the grain, Clark has Silke, and I have three lads standing in Province 3 who'll be hungry again next spring. Anyone who wants that chair filled knows where my price is written: in Rich's fields, one five at a time.",
 "Rob: Three ways stuck again, so the chair stays empty and my soldiers keep eating. When the people double they'll all come round to deal, and I mean to be the price they have to pay.")
act("The year ends with the claims put to the table again. Nobody changes: Clark claims, backed by Silke; Rich and Rob claim; Reina and Paddy stand aside. Rich leads on grain (234.5), Rob on soldiers (3) and Clark on friends (1), and Chaos goes on. Paddy says the first claimant to put a figure on a Province 6 canal and a plan against the Warlord's soldiers gets his name next year.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
# ============================== YEAR 6 ==============================
R(6, "Population Doubles", "double3")
act("Reina, deposed, reads the I Ching herself, unpaid: Population Doubles. Every province has a good harvest, and the population doubles to 200 million, x2, so every province eats twice as much. New land now exists, at 20 a square, but none can be bought under Chaos, with no Emperor to share the cost.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say('Reina', '"Population Doubles. Fat years make large families. All provinces collect a good harvest." The counter now reads two hundred million, so the multiple is x2. Everyone moves down to the second row of their tables, and every mouth in every province now eats twice. This is the year I warned you about. There\'s land to be bought at twenty a square and not one of you can buy it, because nobody is sitting in this chair to share the cost. Clark, the Wall over the north. Write it in.',
 "Reina: Every province's consumption has just doubled and new land is locked behind an empty throne, so Chaos has stopped being cheap. Now they'll bargain in earnest, and my name goes to the first claimant who writes the walls in beside the canals.")
say('Reina', "The people have doubled and the land is waiting, so here are my terms, plainly, for whoever wants them. Province 6's canal first, Province 5's after, the Wall over the north and the Pagoda, all written in the record and with years beside them, and a garrison on the coast so Rob's lads stop carrying off Paddy's furniture. Clark, you're closest. Write the Wall and the Pagoda next to your canals and my name is yours at this year's count. Rich, Rob: you can't afford those terms, and I'll watch you prove it.",
 "Reina: My one friend still wouldn't break this three-way deadlock alone, so I'm putting the price on the table in front of Paddy, who wants a figure. If Clark writes the walls in, Paddy and I can carry him over the line together.")
say('Clark', "Then here it is in writing, with figures, before this harvest is paid. Reina, my first year includes the Wall over the north next to Paddy's canal, and the Pagoda the year after next to Silke's, so put that beside your name. Paddy, the first canal goes to Province 6 in my first year, the first soldiers the crown buys stand on your coast, and you pay half the rate any year a raid or a flood takes your field. Silke, your canal comes in my second year, and the crown pays its full share on every square your coast wants to buy, starting the year I sit. Rich, Rob: that's four works and a guard I've promised. What have either of you written down?",
 "Clark: I can't beat Rich's granary or Rob's swords, but every name I pick up makes it harder for the others to keep refusing, and now Reina and Paddy each have their own price on paper. The Wall and the Pagoda don't cost me a thing to promise, and I'll be glad to deliver them, because walls and pagodas only get built by a man who's actually in the chair.")
say('Rich', "My claim stands, but Her Majesty is right that the wind has changed, so here are my terms, plain enough for Clark to write down. If I'm acclaimed, Province 6's canal goes in during my first year, and I'll buy soldiers out of my own granary to stand on Paddy's coast against Rob's lads. In my second year comes the Wall over the north, and the Pagoda before my reign is out. Paddy, Madam, that's a figure and a promise, and you all know I keep mine to the letter.",
 "Rich: Now the people have doubled, my granary is losing five a year with no land to buy, so a chair I never wanted has turned into the only door to my own fields. If Paddy and Reina put their names on me, I lead on grain and friends together, and I'll happily lay every stone I just promised rather than watch Clark lay them.")
say('Silke', "Here's the count now. Every mouth on my coast eats twice, so an average year leaves me five grain short, and there's land at twenty a square I'm not allowed to buy because that chair's empty. Clark, my name stays on your claim this year, but it's the last year it stays on paper alone. Put in writing that the crown pays its share on the first squares the coast buys, give Paddy his figure and Her Majesty her Wall, and you'll have three names, not one. Rich, the answer's still no.",
 "Silke: Up to now Chaos cost me nothing. With the population doubled I'm losing grain every year, and the only fix is land I can't buy. So I want a chair filled soon, and Clark is the claimant who has to pay for my name, because Rich will never get it.")
say('Paddy', "The people have doubled, and Province 6 is now losing grain every year it can't buy a square, so I'm done standing aside. Clark, you're the only one here who's written down a spade for my province, so my name goes on your claim. Here are my terms, in front of everyone: the canal in Province 6 your first year, new land open to the coast the day you're crowned, and a soldier of the crown's set against Rob's lads on my shore. If you count me short the way you've counted before, I'll take my name back the same year.",
 "Paddy: Rich has the granary and Rob has the swords, but neither of them has offered my coast one spade, so the most I'll ever get out of either is sympathy. Clark's still on paper, but at least it's paper with my canal written on it, and I'll be watching every line he counts.")
say('Rob', "Twenty a square and nobody to sell it, that's the funniest thing I've heard in six years. I'm still claiming. But since everyone's putting figures on the table now, here's mine: whoever wants my swords out of the count pays me for them in hard grain, before he sits down, and keeps his soldiers off my desert. Until somebody names a number, my lads go on eating, and Rich, they're standing one step from your Bowl.",
 "Rob: Now the people have doubled they need a chair, and a chair needs me out of the way, so for the first time my standing aside is worth something. I'll take a good price for it, and if nobody pays, the raids just carry on.")
act("Straight after the card, before the harvest is paid, the claims are put to the table again. Reina, Clark and Rich lay out their terms for the works; Rob names his price for standing aside. Paddy stops standing aside and backs Clark, so Clark now has two friends, Silke and Paddy. The table counts.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say('Reina', "Clark has written the Wall over the north and the Pagoda next to the canals, with years beside them, and that is what I asked for. My name goes on his claim. That's three names, and nobody is anyone's friend for nothing: Clark, I'll hold you to every line, and to Paddy's guard on the coast. Rich, you promised the Wall a year later, out of a granary that was filled while this chair sat empty. Rob, three friends against your three swords still leaves nobody acclaimed, so if you want hard grain for them, name your figure to the man who'll actually be sitting here.",
 "Reina: Clark met my terms, so my name goes to him, but three counts with three different leaders still means Chaos. Someone's claim has to fall now, and Rob is the one who has just said he's for sale.")
say('Clark', "Paddy, your name goes in my ledger and your terms go in beside it, word for word. Province 6's canal in my first year, the coast open to new land the day I'm crowned, and the crown's first soldier on your shore. Silke, the crown pays its share on the first squares your coast buys. Your Majesty, the Wall over the north and the Pagoda go in with years against them: the Wall in my first year with Paddy's canal, the Pagoda in my second with Silke's. That's every term this table has asked for, written down and signed. Rich promised the same list a year too late, and Rob's price is somebody else's grain.",
 "Clark: Two names are on the page and Reina has just told everyone what her third costs, so I've paid it in full and in public. If Rich and Rob still won't step down after that, the whole table will be able to see exactly who is keeping the chair empty.")
say('Rich', "Well, gentlemen, I know which way the wind blows, and it isn't blowing into my granary. I drop my claim and put my name on Clark's, which makes three. Clark, you write everything down, so write this too: the Gentry keeps his seat and the Harvest Pile, the rent stays at the customary five, and the crown pays its full share on Province 3's squares the day the land opens. Do that and you'll find me the most loyal subject this dynasty ever had, Your Majesty.",
 "Rich: With my claim gone, Clark leads on grain and on friends, so he's acclaimed whatever I do. I'd rather he owed the crown to me than notice I was the only one who never helped him. I still don't trust the man, but every promise he just made is now in his own ledger, and I mean to read it back to him every year.")
say('Silke', "My name stays on Clark, and now it's for something written down. Canal for my coast in his second year, the crown's full share on every square we buy starting the year he sits, half rate when we flood. That's three of us on his claim if Her Majesty keeps her word. Clark, I've counted every grain at this table for six years. I'll count yours too, and the first promise you short me on, my name goes back where it came from.",
 "Silke: For the first time, backing Clark actually gets me something: land I can buy this year while the harvest is good, and his terms on the record where Rich can't argue them away. I chose a claimant my landlord can't stand and made him earn it, and Rich still doesn't get my name.")
say('Paddy', "My name stays on Clark. He's put Province 6's canal, the coast guard and the half rate in the record with years beside them, and I'll hold him to every line. Rich, I'll grant you made a fair offer at last, but it came the year after my Jar was gone. Rob, I won't pay you to stop robbing me. Clark, Reina, if it's grain or swords this count still wants, the two of you had better sort it out between you this year, because my coast can't eat another empty chair.",
 "Paddy: Backing Clark gets him the friends, but Rich still has the biggest granary and Rob has the only swords, so one more count has to shift before anyone's crowned. I've done my part in the open, and now Clark has to show he can finish a job and not just write it down.")
say('Rob', "Well, look at that, the clerk's got himself a crowd. Fine. I'm dropping my claim and putting my name on Clark's, and not because I like canals. An empty chair can't pay me to stay home, and an Emperor can. So here's my price, Clark, in your own ledger: what's in my desert stays in my desert, and every year you want my lads kept off your provinces, you pay for it. Rich, that's three names against your granary. Your move, landlord.",
 "Rob: Three years of Chaos and nobody paid me a single grain to behave, so I'll have a man in the chair whose whole job is paying people. He can write my name in his ledger as often as he likes, as long as there's a figure next to it.")
acclamation({2: [1, 3, 5, 6, 8]}, "The acclamation is counted. Rich and Rob drop their claims, and every other player backs Clark: Reina, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob. Clark is the only claimant, so he leads on grain and on friends, and he is acclaimed Emperor. Chaos ends in the sixth year.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say('Rich', "Long live Emperor Clark! The harvest is paid, twenty to each of you, and Your Majesty's twenty goes into the pile, and I'll keep it safe there for you. The rent stays at the customary five, as I promised the day I backed you, and I keep my word to the letter. Your Majesty, since my strip of land only opens under four links of the Wall, I'll be watching that first stretch over the north go up with great interest.",
 "Rich: Five again: I promised it, and a Gentry who keeps his word is a Gentry nobody bothers to replace. The Dragon Jar is back in stock and up for auction this year, and it's the last piece I need, so I'll smile at His Majesty now and bid hard when the Jar comes up.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("Clark, the new Emperor, declares a tax of 30% on this year's harvest, the Winter Palace as the year's work at the crown's cost, 10 to Reina as the Official, and new land: the crown pays the whole 20 for each province's first two squares this year and half of any more, and buys three squares for Province 1.", map_hl=[1])
say('Clark', "My first act goes in the record before anything else. The law won't let a canal be dug in Province 6 or 5 until the Winter Palace stands, so the palace goes up this year at the crown's own cost, Paddy's canal follows next year, and the Wall over the north comes the year after that. Your Majesty, I'm sorry, but I can't build two things in one year. Meanwhile the tax is a light 30%, the crown pays every grain of your first two squares, Paddy, Silke and Rich alike, half on any after that, and the first soldier the crown buys goes to Paddy's shore. Reina, your salary is 10, double the minimum. Rob, you'll get your figure when your lads are back on the sand.",
 "Clark: I promised the canal in my first year and the law makes me lay the palace first, so I've said it out loud before anyone else could put it on my page, and I've paid for the delay in land. Silke and Paddy both said they'd count every grain, so I'm making sure every one they count is in their favour.")
say('Reina', "Six from me, six from Rich, six from Silke, six from Paddy: twenty-four to the crown, counted twice and not a grain short. Rob, your share is nothing, so I won't trouble you for it. Your Majesty, thirty percent on a good harvest is gentle, perhaps gentler than the works can afford. And twelve years ago, I mean half an hour ago, you wrote the Wall over the north into your first year. The Winter Palace has to come first, I know the law better than anyone here, but I'm putting the Wall in my ledger for the year after Paddy's canal, and I'll read it back to you that year.",
 "Reina: For the first time in four years the crown has a tax and a chair, and I'm the one carrying the grain to it. Clark has already slid the Wall back two years, so I'll collect honestly and keep the ledger open on that page.")
taxes(.30, skip=(8,), collector="Reina, now the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Winter Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Winter Palace", at="H20", text="Public works: Clark builds the Winter Palace on H20, at the crown's cost of 20.", hover="Clark pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. The Winter Palace comes before the canals in Provinces 4, 5 and 6.")
buy_land(1, ["F23","F24","F25"], {1:60}, "New land: Clark buys three squares for Province 1 on the eastern coast, F23 to F25, for 60 from the crown's reserve. Each adds 20 to the crown's harvest from next year.")
say('Clark', 'The Winter Palace goes on H20, well back from any sword, and Province 1 takes three squares along the eastern edge. Paddy, buy your soldier and send me the bill. The crown pays the 20, just as I said, so the first soldier this reign pays for stands on your shore.',
 "Clark: The palace and the land are both out of Rob's reach, and paying for Paddy's soldier means I keep the coast-guard promise to the letter even though the law won't let me put a soldier there myself.")
buy_land(5, ["K25","K26","L26","M26","N26","N27"], {5:40, 1:80}, "New land: Silke buys six squares of sea for Province 5, K25, K26, L26, M26, N26 and N27, paying 40; the crown pays 80, the whole 20 on the first two and 10 on each of the rest.")
say('Silke', "Six squares, Your Majesty, on the terms you read out yourself: the crown pays the full twenty on the first two and half on the other four. That's forty from me and eighty from you, all counted. I'm putting them at the north end of the coast, as far from Rob's lads as the law lets me. Next year they put a hundred and twenty back in the harvest, and my canal's in your second year, so I'll be reading that back to you too.",
 "Silke: Six years paying for chairs I'll never sit in, and this is the first time the crown has spent anything on my coast, so I'm buying while it's paying. If the people double again I'd need five squares more just to break even. This way I'm already most of the way there, and I'm keeping my distance from Rob's swords.")
buy_land(6, ["T18","T19","T20","T21","T22"], {6:30, 1:70}, "New land: Paddy buys five squares of the Warlord's land for Province 6, T18 to T22, paying 30; the crown pays 70, the whole 20 on the first two and 10 on each of the rest.")
sell_soldiers([(6, "S24")], "Paddy buys a soldier from Rob for 20 and stands it on S24 in Province 6, beside his Customs House; Rob keeps 5 and 15 goes to the Harvest Pile.")
act("The crown pays Paddy the 20 for his soldier, as Clark promised.", deltas={pid(1):-20, pid(6):20}, deal=True, map_hl=[1,6])
say('Paddy', "Five squares down the coast, Your Majesty, and I'll pay my thirty for them on the nail, so I owe nobody. The soldier goes on S24, right beside my Customs House, where Rob's lad has been standing at my door. Rob, that's your sale for twenty, and from now on my shore answers back. Clark, that's one line of your ledger kept. Next year it's the canal.",
 "Paddy: Every mouth eats twice now, so I need land under me before the next lot of babies arrives, and I've kept the squares well south of Rob's soldier. The guard on S24 is the first thing anybody's actually done for Province 6 instead of promising it, and I'll give Clark credit for it, for now.")
say('Reina', "The Official starts again with an empty table and seventy-four grain, so I'll bid like it. Silke, Paddy, I'll pay fair for your Jar and your Bowl. Rich, you've had six years of the customary five out of the coast. If you want that Jar, you'll pay for it properly.",
 "Reina: I need every piece of a set from scratch, so I'm bidding carefully and keeping twenty back for the set itself. The one man I'll overpay to stop is Rich, because his granary is the last thing this table should be feeding.")
say('Rich', "Your Majesty, I'll bid for that Jar, I won't pretend otherwise, and three quarters of whatever I pay goes into the Harvest Pile, so it feeds every one of you. And let me say it plainly, for the ledger: I put my name to this crown, and the Gentry doesn't tear down what he's just helped build. A full set in my hands is a guarantee for this reign, not a threat to it.",
 "Rich: The Jar completes my set, and a man with a full set is a man everyone is polite to, so I'll pay well for it, and pay more still to keep it from Paddy, who could take my seat at the pile with it. I'm letting the Bowl go, since I already have one and Reina's purse is too thin to finish a set this year anyway.")
say('Silke', "My Jar starts at five. It's only clay, but it's coast clay, and nobody gets it for nothing. And I'm bidding on Paddy's Bowl. Reina, I know you need it too, but you're on seventy-four with a salary of ten, and I've got six new squares to protect. If I lose it, I'll lose it at a fair price, not a cheap one.",
 "Silke: I've finally got land coming in, so I can afford to start putting a set together. I'll go to eighteen for the Bowl and not a grain more, because three quarters of whatever I pay goes into Rich's pile.")
say('Paddy', "I've had one Jar carried off to the desert already, so I'll bid honestly on this one, and not past what a jar is worth. Rich, you offered me twenty-five for mine last year, so you know my price on jars. As for my Bowl, it's five or nothing, and it'll be back again next round if nobody wants it.",
 "Paddy: A Jar puts me one Deed short of a set again, and more to the point it keeps Rich from closing his, so I'll stretch a bit further if he's the one bidding against me. Five on the Bowl is only a modest price, but I'm not handing out a piece of Province 6 for two grain.")
say('Rob', "Long live Emperor Clark, then, and look at him, a palace and three fields before he's even warm. Pass on both, as always; I don't buy furniture, I collect it. Paddy, you paid me for the sword you've stood next to my lad, and you built your fields on my sand, so thank you twice. Clark, 'back on the sand' is a lovely phrase. Put a number next to it and we'll see how fast they walk.",
 "Rob: Six years of passing and I've still got three pieces in my desert I never paid for. And there's a new Winter Palace on H20, a fresh twenty for the crown to lose, only six rounds from W3.")
DRe = "Take it, then. The Official has a long memory and a short purse."
DPa = "That's as far as I go on a jar. I'm not borrowing to buy pottery."
DSi = "Take it, then. I can count, and that's more than it's worth to me."
lot(5, "DJ", [B(2,28), B(2,None,DRe), B(6,35), B(6,None,DPa), B(3,36)], hover="Silke's minimum was 5. Reina would go to 28 only to keep it from Rich; Paddy to 35 to keep it from Rich; Rich's limit was 70. Rich puts the Dragon Jar on I8, and holds a complete set.", at="I8")
say('Rich', "Thirty-six, and twenty-seven of it goes into the Harvest Pile for all of you. Silke, that's a fair price for coast clay, and you won't hear me say otherwise. The Jar goes up in the far north-west corner where Rob's lads would have to walk a long way to admire it. Your Majesty, a full set it may be, but I said it in front of everyone and I'll say it again: it serves your reign, not against it.",
 "Rich: Paddy went to thirty-five to keep it from me, so I paid one past him, and now that's the set made: the Jar, the Bowl and the Customs House. I don't mean to use it, but now everyone knows I could, and from tonight the Emperor, the Official and both peasants will all be very civil to the Gentry.")
say('Silke', "Twenty, Reina. That's my last word on it. Every grain past that goes into Rich's pile, and I've filled that pile enough already.",
 'Silke: One small raise just to see if she blinks. If she goes past twenty, she can have the Bowl and the bill.')
say('Reina', "Twenty-two, Paddy. The Official has to start her set somewhere, and I'd rather start it with a bowl from a coast that answers back.",
 'Reina: Rich took the Jar, so this bowl is my first piece, and twenty-two still leaves me plenty to finish a set later.')
lot(6, "WB", [B(5,18), B(2,18), B(5,20), B(5,None,DSi), B(2,21)], hover="Paddy's minimum was 5. Reina and Silke both reached 18; in one live raise Silke went to 20 and Reina to 22. Reina wins at 21 and puts the Wooden Bowl on G14.", at="G14")
say('Reina', "G14, well back under the line where the Wall ought to stand. Your Majesty, I've put my first piece where your Wall will cover it, so I'll take that as a reason to build it on time.",
 "Reina: G14 keeps the Bowl as far from Rob's lads as Province 2 allows, and it puts my own stake under the Wall that Clark keeps pushing back a year.")
march({"W1":"M12", "W2":"S24", "W3":"L14"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto M12 in Rich's Province 3 and carries off the Wooden Bowl standing there to N12 in the desert, breaking Rich's set; W2 steps straight onto S24 and captures Paddy's new soldier; W3 steps onto L14, raiding 5 from Rich.", take={"W1":("WB","N12")})
say('Rob', "Rich, you had a full set for very nearly a whole afternoon, and I want you to know I enjoyed watching it. Your Bowl's coming to live at N12, and W3 will have his five out of Province 3 as well. Paddy, I sold you that soldier this afternoon and I've just bought him back for nothing, so your shore can answer back from my pocket. Clark, you can see what keeping my lads 'on the sand' costs now. My figure's still waiting for you.",
 "Rob: That's Rich raided, so the only one I've still got to do is Silke, and Reina's new Bowl is sitting at G14 like an invitation. A new crown means someone finally has a reason to pay me, so I'm showing him exactly what he's paying to stop.")
salaries({2:10}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Reina 10 as the Official.")
act("Year 6 ends with Clark on the throne, the Winter Palace built, new land bought for Provinces 1, 5 and 6, and 850 in works still owed.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])

# ===== CHAPTER 4: Fat Year or Thin
# ============================== YEAR 7 ==============================
R(7, "Bad Harvest", "dribble1")
act("Year 7 opens. There's no Scholar, so Clark, as Emperor, appoints Silke to read the I Ching and keep the population counter, for 3 at the end of the year.", map_hl=[1,5])
say('Clark', "Silke reads the card this year. She's the one who wants everything in writing, so she can write down what Heaven says too, and the crown pays her 3 for the ink. Paddy, your canal is still on the page for this year, whatever she turns over.",
 "Clark: I'm handing the card to the hand that's watching my promises closest. If Heaven turns sour, it's a friend who reads it out, not the landlord.")
act("Silke reads the I Ching for the crown: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest, and the population stays at 200 million, x2.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say('Silke', "Written down, as promised: Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame, and it's every province, so nobody gets to feel singled out. Population stays at times two, two hundred million, so read the times-two row and the bad-harvest column, all of you. Clark, a thin year is when a crown earns its keep. Paddy's canal is still on the page, and I'm reading mine right after it.",
 "Silke: A bad year everywhere, so the tax has to come down to fit it, and I'll be counting to check it does. I'd still take a thin harvest with an Emperor over a fat one with nobody in the chair, because only one of those builds me a canal.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say('Rich', "The pile pays out as written: Silke ninety, Paddy seventy, and the rest of us pay into it, myself included, thirty from the Gentry's own granary. And the rent stays at the customary five, bad harvest and all. I said five while I'm Gentry, and a thin year is no time to make a liar of me. Majesty, a lean year is when the works matter most, and Province 3 sits under four links of Wall that would look very handsome with your name carved on them.",
 "Rich: Five from each of them, ten in all. That doesn't cover the thirty I just paid in, but every year I hold it at five is another year Silke can't call me greedy, and I'll want that goodwill when it comes time to share out the cost of the Wall over my land. The canal goes first, and then I start reading Clark's promise back to him.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("The crown's year: Clark declares a tax of 30%, the same as last year. His one work is both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 6, at the crown's cost of 80. The Official's salary is 10, and Silke gets 3 for the reading. On new land the crown pays the whole 20 on each province's first two squares this year and 10 on each beyond.", map_hl=[1,6])
say('Clark', "Thirty percent, the rate I set last year. I won't raise it in a lean year, and I won't cut it while I'm building. Paddy, both sections of your canal go in this year, as written, all 80 from the crown, and that's twenty more grain on your harvest every year from now on. Reina gets her 10, Silke gets her 3, and the land terms stand: the crown pays the whole of your first two squares and half of every one after. Rich, I've seen the Wall above Province 3, and it's in the ledger behind Silke's canal. Come to me with your share and we'll talk about when.",
 "Clark: That's one promise kept to the letter and on time, the first canal in the first full year, and I'm paying for it with the crown's grain, not with a heavier tax. The reserve gets thin after this, so the land I fund is the real plan: every square bought now is grain the treasury can count on later.")
taxes(.30, skip=(8,), collector="Reina, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say('Reina', "Silke, twenty-seven. Paddy, twenty-one. That's forty-eight, counted twice in front of everyone and delivered whole. Clark, it's thin, but it's honest. Paddy's canal goes in today because this chair kept the reserve sealed for four years. Next year the ledger says Silke's canal, and after that the Wall over the north. I wrote that year down the day you moved it, and I'll read it back to you every spring.",
 "Reina: Thirty percent on a bad harvest is gentle, and I'd have taken more, but the crown is building and that's what matters. Every stone that goes up, I'll make sure this table remembers who saved the grain to pay for it, and I'm watching Rich, who suddenly loves the Wall now that it's over his own province.")
build("Grand Canal in Province 6, 2 sections", 80, {1:80}, canal={6:2}, text="Public works: Clark builds both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 6, at the crown's cost of 80.", hover="Clark pays the 80 into the Harvest Pile. Each section adds 10 to Province 6's harvest from next year.")
buy_land(5, ["J25","J26","J27","K27","L27","M27"], {5:40, 1:80}, "New land: Silke buys six more squares of sea for Province 5, J25, J26, J27, K27, L27 and M27, paying 40; the crown pays 80, the whole 20 on the first two and 10 on each of the rest.")
say('Silke', "Six more, all in the north, away from anyone's swords. That's J25, J26, J27, K27, L27 and M27. By Clark's terms the first two are the crown's, twenty each, and on the other four it's ten from me and ten from the crown, so I pay forty and the reserve pays eighty. Counted twice, Reina, the way you like it. Paddy's canal went in today, and I watched it. Mine's next on that page, Clark, and I've got the ink.",
 "Silke: Each square pays for itself in a year, so a bad harvest is exactly when I should be buying, and with a crown back in the chair someone else pays two-thirds of it. Twelve squares of coast by next spring, and I'm still holding back enough grain to bid when the pieces come up.")
buy_land(6, ["T17","U17","U18","U19"], {6:70, 1:10}, "New land: Paddy buys four more squares of the Warlord's land for Province 6, T17, U17, U18 and U19, paying 70; at his own asking the crown pays only 10, since its reserve is nearly spent.")
say('Paddy', "Clark, the canal's in. I watched the spades go into the ground myself, and I'll say it in front of all of you: that's a figure kept. Now the land. Your terms say the crown pays sixty on four squares, but I can count, and after the salaries there's ten and a half in that reserve. So give me ten, two and a half a square, and I'll put up the seventy. Write the other fifty down as owed to Province 6, Silke, ink and all. I'm not letting it go, I just won't have the crown going hungry over my fields.",
 "Paddy: Four squares at the top of my coast, as far from Rob's lad on S24 as I can get them, and each one pays for itself in a year. I'm paying most of it myself because an empty reserve is how we lost the last Emperor, but that fifty goes in the book and I'll ask for it every spring, same as Reina will.")
act("No auctions this year: every province's stock is sold, and nobody has a piece to put up.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
march({"W1":"L11", "W2":"S23", "W3":"K14"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto L11 and W3 onto K14 in Rich's Province 3, raiding 5 from Rich each; W2 crosses Province 6's new canal (a toll of 5 to Paddy) onto S23 and carries off Paddy's Customs House to U21 in the desert.", take={"W2":("CH","U21")})
say('Rob', "Lovely canal, Paddy. I paid your toll to walk along it like a gentleman, and then I took your Customs House home to U21, because a man needs somewhere to keep his receipts. Rich, that's five from K14 and five from L11, bad harvest and all. I'm not one to make a liar of myself either. Majesty, my lads in the north are taking the long way round, and your Summer and Winter Palaces are a few days east of them at twenty a time. I'm told the crown's purse is thirteen and a half. Name me a figure before they get curious, and they'll find the scenery dull.",
 "Rob: W3 is really heading for Reina's Bowl on G14, five rounds away, and that's one of the two pieces I still need. Clark doesn't have to know I'd walk straight past his palaces to get it, and if he's worried about them he can pay me for the walk.")
salaries({2:10}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Reina 10 as the Official.")
act("Clark pays Silke the 3 he promised for reading the I Ching.", deltas={pid(1):-3, pid(5):3}, deal=True, map_hl=[1,5])
act("Year 7 ends with both sections of the Province 6 canal built, ten more squares of new land bought for Provinces 5 and 6, the crown's reserve down to 0.5, and 770 in works still owed.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
# ============================== YEAR 8 ==============================
R(8, "Storms and Raiders", "repairs")
act("Year 8 opens. Silke reads the I Ching again as the crown's appointee.", map_hl=[1,5])
act("Silke reads the I Ching: Storms and Raiders. Every province collects a good harvest; the population stays at 200 million, x2. Provinces 5 and 6 pay 2 each to repair their works, and Provinces 1, 2, 3 and 8 play a personal disaster.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say('Silke', "Storms and Raiders. Good harvest everywhere, and I've written that one down with pleasure. Population stays at times two, two hundred million, so it's the times-two row again. Provinces 4, 5, 6 and 7 pay two each to mend their works, and I'll pay my two without a fuss. Provinces 1, 2, 3 and 8, turn the next page of your booklets, your personal disasters are up. Clark, the crown has half a grain in it and my canal is next on the page, so a good year had better fill that reserve before anyone starts naming figures to Rob.",
 "Silke: A good harvest with a thirty percent tax gets the crown back from half a grain to something it can build with, and I'd sooner it went into my canal than into Rob's protection money. I'll pay a fair tax this year, and I'll be counting where every grain of it goes.")
act("Repairs: Silke and Paddy each pay 2 to the Harvest Pile.", deltas={pid(5):-2, pid(6):-2}, pile_delta=4, map_hl=[5,6])
disaster(1, "Clark pays 10, out of the crown's reserve.")
disaster(2, "Reina pays 10.")
disaster(3, "Rich pays 10.")
disaster(8, "Rob pays 5, and may raid nothing at all this year.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say('Rich', "A good year at last, and the pile pays out as written: Silke two hundred and sixty, Paddy two hundred and twenty, twenty each to Reina and myself, forty to the crown. The rent stays at the customary five, fat year or thin. Majesty, you told me to come with my share, so here I am: when the Wall over Province 3 goes on the page, the Gentry will put up a quarter of every link out of his own granary. Write it down, Reina. I'd like to see my name on that stone beside the crown's.",
 "Rich: Twenty in from the harvest, ten from rent, and Rob's lads are stuck at home this year, so I'm finally gaining ground. My land can't earn a grain until that Wall stands, and the crown has half a grain in its purse, so a quarter from me is the cheapest way to move it up the page and get it remembered.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("The crown's year: Clark declares a tax of 30% again. His one work is both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 5, at the crown's cost of 80. He will pay Paddy the 50 left owed on last year's land, and on new land this year the crown pays 10 a square. The Official's salary is 10, and Silke gets 3 for the reading. The Wall over the north, which he had promised for this year, goes on the page for next year.", map_hl=[1,5,6])
say('Clark', "Thirty percent again. Same rate, fat year or thin, so nobody can say the crown grabs when the barns are full. Silke, both sections of your canal go in this year, all from the crown, in the order it was written in Year 4: Province 6 first, then Province 5. Paddy, the fifty you let the crown owe gets paid this year, every grain of it. Reina, write it down as settled. Land is ten a square from the crown this year, for everyone. Rich, your quarter is in the ledger: the Wall goes on the page next year, the north first, with your stone and Reina's beside mine. And Rob, the crown doesn't pay for scenery. My palaces are six rounds from your lads, and the table's grain is building canals.",
 "Clark: Two canals in two years, and the one debt I owed is cleared. That's the kind of record that makes a table tax itself without grumbling. I've moved the Wall a year, and I said so in the open with the Gentry's money already on it, so it reads as a schedule and not as a broken promise.")
taxes(.30, skip=(8,), collector="Reina, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say('Reina', "Six from my own bowl first, so nobody says the collector spares herself. Then Rich six, Silke seventy-eight, Paddy sixty-six: a hundred and fifty-six, counted twice and delivered whole. Clark, that pays Silke's canal, Paddy's fifty and our salaries, with something left over in the reserve. Paddy's debt goes down as settled the moment it's paid. And Rich, your quarter is in the ledger beside the Wall. But Majesty, in Year 6 you wrote the Wall for Year 8, and this is Year 8. I've written Year 9 next to it in ink, and that's the last time I move it.",
 "Reina: A good harvest, and for once the crown can pay for everything it promised in a single season. That's what a tax does when it's actually collected. The Wall has slipped twice now, and I'm letting it go this once because the canals are real, but next spring I'll read that page out loud to the whole table.")
build("Grand Canal in Province 5, 2 sections", 80, {1:80}, canal={5:2}, text="Public works: Clark builds both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 5, at the crown's cost of 80.", hover="Clark pays the 80 into the Harvest Pile. Each section adds 10 to Province 5's harvest from next year.")
act("The crown pays Paddy the 50 he left owed on last year's four squares of land, as Clark promised.", deltas={pid(1):-50, pid(6):50}, deal=True, map_hl=[1,6])
buy_land(5, ["I25","I26","I27","I28"], {5:70, 1:10}, "New land: Silke buys four more squares of sea for Province 5, I25 to I28, paying 70; at her own asking the crown pays only 10 of the 40 it offered, and nothing is left owed.")
say('Silke', "My canal's in. Year 4's page, read back and kept, and I'll write 'built' next to it myself. Four more squares, I25 to I28: eighty in all, seventy from me and ten from the crown, two and a half a square, the same as Paddy took last year. And nothing written down as owed, Clark. I don't want that thirty back. I want it to stay in the reserve so the Wall goes up in Year 9 and Reina never has to move that date again.",
 "Silke: Sixteen squares of coast and a canal on it, and that's worth more to me than thirty grain of subsidy, especially if the subsidy empties the purse the next work has to come out of. A crown with grain in it doesn't get a Warlord bargaining for its palaces, and it doesn't hand the next wobble to Rich.")
buy_land(6, ["T16","U16","V16","V17","V18"], {6:87.5, 1:12.5}, "New land: Paddy buys five more squares for Province 6, T16, U16, V16, V17 and V18, paying 87.5; at his own asking the crown pays only 12.5, and nothing is left owed.")
act("No auctions this year: every province's stock is sold, and nobody has a piece to put up.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say('Paddy', "Clark, the fifty came back the year you said it would. Reina, write it settled, and I'll write it in my own book too. Five more squares up the top of the coast, and I'll take the same as Silke, two and a half a square and nothing owed. Keep the rest in the reserve for the Wall over the north. I lost my House to Rob this spring, so the crown's not the one I'm worried about now.",
 "Paddy: Rob's lad took my last piece off S23, so for now my land is all I hold, and every square I buy is twenty more grain a year that can't be carried off to the desert. I'm keeping these five as far up the coast from his soldier as I can get them, and leaving the crown its grain, because an Emperor who pays his debts back on time is worth keeping fed.")
march({"W3":"J14"}, "Rob marches, but his disaster forbids any raid this year: W1 and W2 stand still, and W3 walks onto J14 in Reina's Province 2, raiding nothing.")
say('Rob', "Heaven's told my lads to keep their hands in their pockets this year, so they're taking a stroll and nothing more. Reina, that one in your field is only sightseeing. Clark, you're right, the crown doesn't pay for scenery. It's a good thing the scenery's free this year, then. Next year I'll send you the bill.",
 "Rob: No raids anywhere this year, so W3 just walks into Province 2, three rounds off Reina's Bowl, and she gets a whole year to watch him coming. I've let Clark think it's the palaces I'm after, and I'd like him to keep thinking it.")
salaries({2:10}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Reina 10 as the Official.")
act("Clark pays Silke 3 for reading the I Ching.", deltas={pid(1):-3, pid(5):3}, deal=True, map_hl=[1,5])
act("Year 8 ends with both sections of the Province 5 canal built, the crown's old land debt to Paddy paid, nine more squares of new land for Provinces 5 and 6, 21 in the crown's reserve, and 690 in works still owed. The Wall over the north is promised for next year.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])

# ===== CHAPTER 5: Counted Into the Mortar
# ============================== YEAR 9 ==============================
R(9, "The Emperor's Share", "share")
act("Year 9 opens. Silke reads the I Ching again as the crown's appointee.", map_hl=[1,5])
act("Silke reads the I Ching: The Emperor's Share. Every province collects an average harvest; the population stays at 200 million, x2. Each province with a Public Works pays the Emperor half the extra harvest it produces this round: the canals in Provinces 5 and 6 each add 20, so Silke and Paddy each owe Clark 10.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say('Silke', "\"The Emperor's Share. The Emperor would like to remind you who paid for the ditch. Average harvest for every province, and any province with a Public Works splits the extra that work grows fifty-fifty with the Emperor this round.\" The counter stays at two hundred million, so it's times two, same row as last year. Read your tables off the x2 line. Paddy, that's you and me with the canals, so half of what our water grows goes up the road. Clark, I helped pay for that ditch and I can count. Every grain of my half goes into the Wall this year, not into anything with a roof on it.",
 "Silke: Fine. Heaven wants half of what my canal grows, and I'll hand it over, but only because the crown is two grain from broke and the Wall is on the page for Year 9. If it shows up as a new cushion on Clark's chair, I'll know.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
act("The Emperor's Share: Silke and Paddy each pay Clark 10, half of what their canals add to the harvest.", deltas={pid(5):-10, pid(6):-10, pid(1):20}, map_hl=[1,5,6])
say('Rich', "The pile pays out as the card says: Clark fifteen, Silke three hundred and thirty-five, Paddy two hundred and ninety-five, and Reina, Rob and I each put our little bit back in. Rent is the customary five from each of you, the same as it's been every year, fat or thin. And Silke, that's handsomely said about your half going to the Wall. My quarter is counted and waiting, ten a link, and Reina, I'll be very glad to hear you read that line out when we come to the works.",
 "Rich: Five is a small price for nine years of people saying the Gentry keeps his word. With Silke throwing her canal money at the Wall too, this is the year my four links finally get laid, and once they're up my strip opens and I have land to farm at last.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("The crown's year: Clark declares a tax of 50%. His one work is the Great Wall: ten links this year, all four above Province 1 and all four above Province 3, and the first two above Province 2, 400 in all. The crown pays 340; he asks Rich for 10 a link on Province 3's four (40) and Reina for 10 a link on Province 2's two (20). On new land the crown pays 5 a square this year. The Official's salary is 10.", map_hl=[1,2,3])
say('Clark', "Year 9, the Wall, as written in ink and not moved again. I'm asking fifty this once, because two canals now pay Silke and Paddy more in a year than the whole table had in Year 2, and I won't lay one link and call that a Wall. Ten links go up this year: Province 1 whole, Province 3 whole on Rich's quarter, and Reina, two of yours on ten a link, with the last two first in line next year. Silke, your ten goes in the mortar. I'm writing it down with your name on it.",
 "Clark: Fifty percent of three hundred is a hard sum to hear, but a thin Wall is no protection, and nobody revolts over a rate when the stone is going up where they can see it. Rich has paid his quarter, so for the first time in nine years the landlord's page has more in the credit column than the debit, and I'll be watching to see if he likes that or not.")
taxes(.50, skip=(8,), collector="Reina, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say('Reina', "Three hundred and fifteen, counted twice: Silke one sixty-seven and a half, Paddy one forty-seven and a half, and not a grain lost on the road. Clark, here's Province 2's twenty for its own two links. I've waited nine years to pay for stone over my own roof, so I'm not going to haggle it down now. And yes, Rich, I'll read your forty out loud when the mortar's wet and not a moment before. You'll hear me read Year 10 beside my last two links as well.",
 "Reina: Fifty percent on a fat year is exactly what I asked for in Year 2, and they burned my chair for it. Now the same table pays it, and I'm putting my own grain under the north. Twenty out of fifty-two hurts, but this is the first year since I lost the throne that the money turns into a wall and not into a promise.")
say('Rich', "Forty, Your Majesty, ten a link, exactly as I put it on the table last year and exactly as Reina wrote it down. Here it is, counted out before she's finished reading. And if the masons need a name to cut into the stone above Province 3, I wouldn't say no to Baron.",
 "Rich: Forty grain gets me a hundred and sixty grain's worth of Wall, and it opens my strip for land this same year, so it's the best price I've paid since that Customs House. Even better, the whole table watched the Gentry keep his word to the grain.")
build("Great Wall, ten links", 400, {1:340, 3:40, 2:20}, wall={1:4, 3:4, 2:2}, text="Public works: the Great Wall goes up, ten links in one year: all four above Province 1, all four above Province 3, and the first two above Province 2. The crown pays 340, Rich 40 and Reina 20.", hover="400 into the Harvest Pile. With four links above them, Provinces 1 and 3 may now buy squares on the strip below the Wall. Province 2 needs its last two links first.")
buy_land(3, ["G9","G10","G11"], {3:45, 1:15}, "New land: Rich buys the first three squares of Province 3's strip below the new Wall, G9, G10 and G11, paying 45; the crown pays its 5 a square, 15.")
say('Rich', "Three squares under the new stone, Your Majesty: G9, G10 and G11. Forty-five from the Gentry and the crown's five a square, which is fifteen, and I'll take it gratefully. Nine years I've paid into that pile and taken nothing out of the ground. From next year Province 3 finally puts something back.",
 "Rich: Sixty grain of land that grows sixty a year, and I'm only paying forty-five of it, so it's paid for before the next drought arrives. I'm watching that lad of Rob's on L11, because new fields are just more grain for him to eat, and some of this granary is going on swords soon.")
buy_land(5, ["H25","H26","H27","H28","J28","K28"], {5:114, 1:6}, "New land: Silke buys six more squares of sea for Province 5, H25 to H28, J28 and K28, paying 114; at her own asking the crown pays only 6 of the 30 it offered, and nothing is left owed.")
say('Silke', "Six squares, a hundred and twenty. I pay a hundred and fourteen and the crown pays one a square, six. Clark, you offered thirty and you've got thirty-one in the purse with Reina's ten still to pay, so I'm not taking it. Fair point on the two grain, Storyteller: it was twenty-one. That's still not a purse, it's a pocket. Write the six down as the crown's share anyway, and next year, with ten links of Wall standing, I'll remember who laid the mortar.",
 "Silke: That's twenty-two squares of coast. They'll be paying back a hundred and twenty a year from next harvest, and the crown still has enough left to pay its Official. I want a crown that can afford the last two hundred and ninety, not one that bankrupts itself handing me five-grain coins.")
act("Storyteller's note, to the table: the Emperor must pay some part of every square of new land, and pay it at the time, not owe it (Section 13). The smallest part is half a grain a square, so on Paddy's five squares the crown pays 2.5 and Paddy 97.5.", map_hl=[1,6])
buy_land(6, ["T15","U15","V15","V19","V20"], {6:97.5, 1:2.5}, "New land: Paddy buys five more squares for Province 6, T15, U15, V15, V19 and V20, paying 97.5; the crown pays the smallest share the rule allows, half a grain a square.")
say('Paddy', "Five squares up the top of the coast, a hundred grain, and I'll pay the lot myself. Your Majesty, you've ten in the purse and Reina's ten still to pay, and I'll not be the man who leaves the Official unpaid in the year the Wall went up. Last year you paid me back fifty the spring you said you would, and that's worth more to me than five a square. Keep your purse for the stone. Two hundred and ninety still owed is two hundred and ninety, and I'd rather see it standing than see it in my granary.",
 "Paddy: I'm buying on the north edge, away from Rob's lad on S23, and I'm buying more land rather than pieces because soil can't be carried off to the desert. I'm letting the crown off the share on purpose, too: an Emperor who can pay his Official this year will still be on the throne to finish the Wall next year.")
act("No auctions this year: every province's stock is sold, and nobody has a piece to put up.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
march({"W1":"L10", "W2":"R22", "W3":"I14"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto L10 in Rich's Province 3 and carries off the Customs House standing there to L8 in the desert; W2 steps onto R22 in Paddy's Province 6, raiding 5 from Paddy; W3 steps onto I14 in Reina's Province 2, raiding 5 from Reina, two rounds from her Wooden Bowl on G14.", take={"W1":("CH","L8")})
say('Rob', "Lovely bit of stone, Your Majesty, ten links of it, and every one of them looks very nice from where my lads are standing. Rich, I'm borrowing your Customs House. The desert's short of a toll booth. Paddy, the usual five, and Reina, five from you too, since my lad's in your field and it'd be rude to leave without paying a call. Clark, I'm still waiting on that figure. A Wall's no use if the men on the wrong side of it are the ones you never paid.",
 "Rob: That makes six pieces on my sand, and W3 is two steps off Reina's Bowl now. Everyone's watching the Wall and the palaces, so nobody's watching her kitchen. Seven and a half grain in the crown's purse means Clark can't pay me this year, so I'll take my pay off his friends instead.")
salaries({2:5}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Reina 5 as the Official, the minimum, with the crown's reserve at 7.5; he promises her the other 5 first out of next year's tax.")
act("Clark pays Silke 2.5 for reading the I Ching.", deltas={pid(1):-2.5, pid(5):2.5}, deal=True, map_hl=[1,5])
say('Clark', "Seven and a half in the drawer, and I'm not putting the crown in debt in the year the Wall went up. Reina, you get five tonight and the other five comes first out of next year's tax, before a single stone is ordered. It's written down as owed to you, in my hand. Silke, two and a half for the reading, and that empties the purse. Rob, here's your figure: nothing. You can see for yourself there's nothing left in it.",
 "Clark: Ten links up and an empty treasury, and I'd sooner have it that way round than the other. The five I owe Reina is the only debt on my page, I've named the day I'll pay it, and that's how you stop a debt becoming a cause for revolt.")
act("Year 9 ends with ten links of the Great Wall built (Provinces 1 and 3 whole, Province 2 half), fourteen squares of new land bought for Provinces 3, 5 and 6, the crown's reserve at 0, and 290 in works still owed: Province 2's last two Wall links, the Province 1 canal, the Province 3 Reservoir, the Province 2 Irrigation System and the Marble Pagoda.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
# ============================== YEAR 10 ==============================
R(10, "The Canal Gives Way", "canal")
act("Year 10 opens. Silke reads the I Ching again as the crown's appointee.", map_hl=[1,5])
act("Silke reads the I Ching: The Canal Gives Way. Every province collects an average harvest; the population stays at 200 million, x2. No canal stands half-built, so no canal work is lost. Provinces 5 and 6 each have both canal sections, so Silke and Paddy each pay the Emperor 2. Provinces 2, 3 and 8 play a personal disaster (Province 7 is empty).", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say('Silke', "\"The Canal Gives Way. The engineers blame the river. The river declines to comment.\" Average harvest for everyone, and any unfinished canal work is lost. Where both sections are finished, that province pays the Emperor two. Where only one stands, the Emperor builds it again. Provinces 2, 3, 7 and 8 each play a Personal Disaster, so Reina, Rich, turn the next page in your booklets. The counter stays at two hundred million, times two, same row as last year. Clark, both my sections held, so I'm paying you two for a ditch that didn't break. That's the first time I've been fined for good workmanship, and that two goes on top of the two and a half you paid me for reading it, so we're about square.",
 "Silke: A canal that holds costs me two, and an Emperor with an empty drawer gets four from the coast before the tax is even counted. I'll pay it gladly if it keeps Reina's last two links on the page for this year.")
act("The canals hold: Silke and Paddy each pay Clark 2.", deltas={pid(5):-2, pid(6):-2, pid(1):4}, map_hl=[1,5,6])
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
disaster(2, "Reina pays half the round's income, and her income is negative, so she pays nothing.")
disaster(3, "Rich pays half his grain.")
disaster(8, "Rob pays half his grain.")
say('Rich', "The pile pays out as read: Clark fifteen, Silke four hundred and fifty-five, Paddy three hundred and ninety-five, Province 3 fifty-five, the first fat harvest it's ever had, and Reina and Rob put theirs back in. Rent is the customary five each, and yes, Silke, the same five in the year Heaven took half my granary. I said five and I meant five. Rob, I hope that toll booth gives you splinters.",
 "Rich: Half my grain is gone to Heaven and the Customs House to the desert, so I'm a man with eighty-eight grain and one Jar, and asking for more rent now would only look like panic. Those three new squares will fill the granary back up, and the first grain I spare goes on a sword to guard them, and then on the Reservoir.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("The crown's year: Clark declares a tax of 30%. He will first pay Reina the 5 he owed her from last year. His one work is the last two links of the Great Wall, above Province 2, 80 in all: the crown 60, and he asks Reina for 10 a link (20). On new land the crown pays 10 a square this year, and it buys four squares for Province 1 on its strip below the Wall, paying all 80. The Official's salary is 10.", map_hl=[1,2])
say('Clark', "First the five I owe Reina, paid before anything else, as I said it would be. Then the last two links over Province 2, Reina's ten a link and the crown's sixty, and when the mortar's dry there's a Wall from Province 1 to the desert. I promised it in Year 8 and laid it in Years 9 and 10. Back to thirty this year, because last year was for stone and this year the purse has to be refilled, and the crown pays ten a square on new land and doesn't ask anyone to wave it off.",
 "Clark: Nobody left in the ledger can say the Wall was delayed, the crown owes no one, and the provinces paid their share of the stone over their own roofs. After this there's 210 still owed, the canal, the reservoir, the irrigation and the Pagoda, and for the first time I've got a full purse to pay for them.")
taxes(.30, skip=(8,), collector="Reina, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
act("The crown pays Reina the 5 it owed her from last year's salary, first out of the tax, as Clark promised.", deltas={pid(1):-5, pid(2):5}, deal=True, map_hl=[1,2])
say('Reina', "Two hundred seventy-one and a half, counted twice. Rich sixteen and a half, Silke one thirty-six and a half, Paddy one eighteen and a half, and nobody's short. Clark, you paid the five you owed me before anything else, as you said you would, and I'm writing 'settled' beside it. Here's Province 2's twenty for the last two links. When that mortar's dry I'll read the whole line out, Year 8 to Year 10, and I'll strike the Wall off the ledger in my own hand. Rob, you're welcome to my five. Admire the stone on your way out, because from now on it's between you and the north.",
 "Reina: Twenty out of twenty-seven leaves me close to empty, and it's the best grain I've spent since Year 1, because the strip under that Wall is my land and it opens the moment the last stone sits. The Pagoda's next on that page, and Clark knows I'll be reading it to him.")
build("Great Wall, the last two links", 80, {1:60, 2:20}, wall={2:2}, text="Public works: the last two links of the Great Wall go up above Province 2, the crown paying 60 and Reina 20. The Great Wall stands whole, all twelve links, from Province 1 to the desert.", hover="80 into the Harvest Pile. Province 2's strip below the Wall is now open for new land.")
buy_land(1, ["E19","E20","E21","E22"], {1:80}, "New land: Clark buys four squares for Province 1 on its strip below the new Wall, E19 to E22, the crown paying all 80.")
say('Clark', "Four squares under the new stone for Province 1, E19 to E22, and the crown pays its own twenty a square like anyone else. Reina, put it in the ledger next to everyone else's, because I don't keep one set of books for the table and another for myself.",
 "Clark: Those four squares sit right under the Wall and well away from the Warlord's lads, and from next year they're eighty a harvest the crown never had. That's the reservoir and the canal paid for without asking the coast for another grain.")
buy_land(2, ["F11"], {2:10, 1:10}, "New land: Reina buys the first square of Province 2's strip, F11, paying 10; the crown pays its 10.")
say('Reina', "One square, F11, under my own Wall. Ten from me and ten from the crown at the rate Clark named, and I'm asking for nothing more. I put my last full twenty into the stone, so that's all Province 2 can do this year. Next year it'll be more, and the year after that the ring goes all the way along the Wall. Clark, the Pagoda is the next line on your page. I'll be reading it to you.",
 "Reina: Ten out of twelve leaves me two grain and a salary on the way, and I'd rather have one square of my own under the Wall than a fat purse and a bare strip. F11 is as far as I can get from Rob's lad on I14 for now.")
say('Rich', "The next ring out, Your Majesty: F9 to F12, four squares. Forty from me and the crown's ten a square, which is forty, and thank you for not making me beg for it. Reina, you can write it down: a Wall from Province 1 to the desert, and Province 3 farming behind it for the first time in ten years.",
 "Rich: It costs me forty and comes back as eighty a year, and seven squares means a fourth population card can't put me back in the red. That leaves me forty-two, which is just about the price of a sword to keep Rob's lads off my new fields, and after that the Reservoir is the next stone with my name on it.")
act("Rich and Reina both named F11; Province 2 comes first in table order, so F11 is Reina's, and Rich chooses E12 instead.", map_hl=[2,3])
buy_land(3, ["F9","F10","F12","E12"], {3:40, 1:40}, "New land: Rich buys four more squares on Province 3's strip, F9, F10, F12 and E12, paying 40; the crown pays its 10 a square, 40.")
say('Rich', "F11 is yours, Reina, with my compliments, and good neighbours make a good Wall. I'll take E12 instead, behind my own F12. It's still four squares, forty from me and forty from the crown, as before.",
 "Rich: One square of mine went to the Official, which is what you'd expect from a woman who reads the order of the table out loud. E12 grows just as much grain as F11 would have, and it reaches deeper into my own strip.")
buy_land(5, ["G27","G28","G29","H29","I29","J29","K29","L28"], {5:120, 1:40}, "New land: Silke buys eight more squares of sea for Province 5, G27 to G29, H29, I29, J29, K29 and L28, paying 120; at her own asking the crown pays 40, half its offer.")
say('Silke', "Eight squares on the north edge of the coast, a hundred and sixty. I pay a hundred and twenty, the crown pays forty, five a square, and I'm not waving it off, Clark, I'm halving it. Count it up: two twenty-five and a half in the drawer, eighty on your own strip, forty to me, and Reina's ten, so you've still got about ninety going into next year. There's two hundred and ten still owed, and I'd sooner my other forty went into that than into my fields. The Wall's whole, Reina, and I'll say it out loud: three years, on the page, not moved.",
 "Silke: That's thirty squares of coast now, all on the north side and well away from Rob's lads. Next year the coast pays a hundred and sixty more, and that crown has the purse to start on what's left of the two hundred and ten.")
buy_land(6, ["T14","U14","V14","U20","V21"], {6:75, 1:25}, "New land: Paddy buys five more squares for Province 6, T14, U14, V14, U20 and V21, paying 75; at his own asking the crown pays 25, half its offer.")
say('Paddy', "Five more squares, Your Majesty: T14, U14, V14, U20 and V21. You offered ten a square and you meant it, I know that. But you've paid back every grain you owed me and Reina, and there's still two hundred and ten of work owed on that ledger. So I'll take five a square, not ten, and I'm not waving it off, I'm asking for it. Put the other twenty-five toward whatever goes up next. A Wall from Province 1 to the desert, built when you said it would be. I'll pay my tax for that without a word.",
 "Paddy: The crown's paid its debts and kept its figures, so I'm leaving a bit more in its purse for the last of the works. I'd sooner have an Emperor who can build than an extra twenty-five in my granary. Every square I buy is soil, and Rob's lads can raid it but they can't carry it off.")
act("No auctions this year: every province's stock is sold, and nobody has a piece to put up.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
sell_back(pid(2), ["WB"], "W3's raid on H14 takes 5 from Reina, and she holds 2, so before she pays she must sell realia back to the Harvest Pile at 2 each (Note 7): her Wooden Bowl lifts off G14 into the pile's own stock, to be auctioned at the next auction.", hover="Four grain doesn't cover the 5; the last 1 is a debt. In debt she can't bid, buy, trade, give grain or change seats, and every bag she receives pays the debt first. Debt is a cause for revolt.")
march({"W1":"K9", "W2":"Q21", "W3":"H14"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto K9 in Rich's Province 3, raiding 5 from Rich; W2 steps onto Q21 in Paddy's Province 6, raiding 5 from Paddy; W3 steps onto H14 in Reina's Province 2, raiding 5 from Reina, one step from her Wooden Bowl on G14.")
say('Rob', "A Wall from Province 1 to the desert, and it's lovely. Mind you, it faces north, and my lads are already on this side of it. Clark, you've thirty and a half in the drawer now, so 'nothing' isn't your figure any more. Reina, I'll take your five, or whatever you've got. Rich, the splinters are worth it.",
 "Rob: W3 is one step off Reina's Bowl, and W2 is heading up the coast for Silke's fat barns. That's the last two names on my list. If Clark wants my lads kept off his palaces, he'll have to offer me something before they walk back from the north.")
salaries({2:10}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Reina 10 as the Official; the first grain of it clears her debt.")
act("Clark pays Silke 3 for reading the I Ching.", deltas={pid(1):-3, pid(5):3}, deal=True, map_hl=[1,5])
say('Clark', "Reina, your ten as declared, and the first grain of it clears the debt Rob's lad put you in, so nobody at this table goes into Year 11 owing anything. Silke, three for the reading. Rob, you're right, there's seventeen and a half in the drawer now. It's for the Pagoda and the reservoir, and it's going to stay in the drawer, not go to the desert. Every grain you've taken off Reina is on your page, with the square beside it.",
 "Clark: A debt at this table is a cause for revolt, so I've paid off Reina's before anyone could think to say the word. Rob's on page six now, and the more he raids an Official who's just paid for her own Wall, the fewer friends he'll have left when he needs them.")
act("Year 10 ends with the Great Wall whole, all twelve links, twenty-two more squares of new land across all five provinces, Reina's Wooden Bowl in the Harvest Pile's stock, 17.5 in the crown's reserve, and 210 in works still owed: the Province 1 canal, the Province 3 Reservoir, the Province 2 Irrigation System and the Marble Pagoda.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])

# ===== CHAPTER 6: The Ink Holds
# ============================== YEAR 11 ==============================
R(11, "Fortune and Misfortune", "goodevil")
act("Year 11 opens. Silke reads the I Ching again as the crown's appointee.", map_hl=[1,5])
act("Silke reads the I Ching: Fortune and Misfortune. Every province collects a good harvest; the population stays at 200 million, x2. Provinces 2, 6 and 8 play a personal disaster (Province 4 is empty).", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say('Silke', "\"Fortune and Misfortune. Plenty for everyone, and trouble for some.\" A good harvest in every province, so mine too. Provinces 2, 4, 6 and 8 each play a Personal Disaster, which means Reina and Paddy turn the next page of their booklets. Four and Eight are empty, so they get off free. The counter still reads two hundred million, times two, so read the same row you read last year. And Clark, a good harvest means a good tax, so you'll have no excuse when I ask where the canal is.",
 "Silke: Heaven skipped Province 5 again, and I'm sitting on a full harvest from thirty squares of coast while Paddy, the only one close to my grain, has to take a disaster. I'll pay my share into the canal, but I'll be counting it, because at the end of this it's the grain left in my hand that settles the score.")
act("Storyteller's note, to the table: Province 4 is empty, but Province 8 is Rob's, so the Warlord plays his disaster too.", map_hl=[8])
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
disaster(2, "Reina pays 4.")
disaster(6, "Paddy pays 10.")
disaster(8, "Rob's soldiers may raid nothing in Provinces 5, 6 and 7 this year.")
say('Rich', "A good harvest in every province, and the pile pays every man in full: a hundred and twenty to the crown, forty to our Official, five hundred and twenty to Paddy, six hundred and forty to Silke, and a hundred and sixty to my own new fields, the first Province 3 has ever earned. Rent's the customary five, Silke, Paddy. Eleven years now, and I said five and it's still five. And Clark, since the Wall's done, I'll say it before anyone else does: the Reservoir is Province 3's stone, and the Gentry will put his grain toward it the same way he did for the Wall.",
 "Rich: A hundred and sixty out of the ground and ten in rent, and I'm back near two hundred and seventy. That's enough to buy a sword to stand next to my Jar and still pay my share of the Reservoir. Silke and Paddy are sitting on fifteen hundred between them, so I'll squeeze them later, when they come asking a favour. For now my word is worth more than they could pay me.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("The crown's year: Clark declares a tax of 30%. His one work is the Province 3 Reservoir, 80: the crown 60, and he asks Rich for 20, a quarter, as on his Wall links. He names the order of the rest: the Province 1 canal next year, then the Irrigation System, then the Marble Pagoda. On new land the crown pays the full 20 on every square bought this year, and Province 1 buys four squares of its own. The Official's salary is 10, and Silke gets 3 for the reading.", map_hl=[1,3])
say('Clark', "Thirty percent again. That's four hundred and eight, and I didn't move the rate in a fat year either, so write that down, Silke. Rich asked first and he's paid first before, so the Reservoir goes in this year: sixty from the crown and your usual quarter, twenty. The Province 1 canal comes next year, then the Irrigation, then the Pagoda in a year with nothing else beside it. That's one a year until the list is empty, and Reina can strike them off as they go. And any square bought this year, the crown pays the whole twenty.",
 "Clark: With a full drawer I can be generous, and paying for everyone's land gets every province's name into my ledger on the credit side. Four works left and four years to build them, and I plan to be sitting in this chair the day Reina crosses off the last one.")
taxes(.30, skip=(8,), collector="Reina, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say('Reina', "Four hundred and eight, counted twice: Silke one hundred ninety-two, Paddy one hundred fifty-six, Rich forty-eight, and my own twelve first, before anyone else's. Rob, you owe nothing, so there's nothing to refuse. Clark, I'll strike the Reservoir off when the stone's set, and I'm writing the Irrigation into Year 13 beside the canal in my own hand, so it doesn't slip the way the Wall did. And Rich, the Wall was my idea eleven years before it was yours to pay a quarter of.",
 "Reina: The crown's paying the whole twenty, so three squares under my Wall cost me nothing, and they'll harvest every year from next year on. The Irrigation in Province 2 is third on the list now, and I'll be sitting in this chair when it goes in.")
say('Rich', "Twenty from Province 3 for the Reservoir, my quarter, the same as I paid on every Wall link. Count it now, Reina, before you strike anything off. Since you've taken E11 and F13 first, I'll have E13, and I gladly accept the crown's twenty for it. And Reina, you can have the Wall's idea. I'll settle for having paid for it.",
 "Rich: Twenty buys my quarter of the Reservoir, and it pays me twenty a year from next harvest, so it's paid for itself by Year 12. The crown is paying the whole cost of E13, so it's free ground. That leaves a hundred and thirty-nine in the granary to buy a sword before Rob's lad on K9 takes an interest in my new fields.")
build("Province 3 Reservoir", 80, {1:60, 3:20}, key="res3", at="H9:I11", text="Public works: the Province 3 Reservoir goes in on H9 to I11, beside Rich's Dragon Jar, the crown paying 60 and Rich 20.", hover="80 into the Harvest Pile. The Reservoir adds 20 to Province 3's harvest from next year, and a Warlord soldier crossing it pays a toll. Three works are still owed: the Province 1 canal, the Province 2 Irrigation System and the Marble Pagoda.")
buy_land(1, ["E23","E24","F26","F27"], {1:80}, "New land: Clark buys four more squares for Province 1, E23, E24, F26 and F27, the crown paying all 80.")
say('Clark', "My mistake, those F squares were mine already, and I'll put that in the ledger against myself. Province 1 takes E23, E24, F26 and F27 instead, four squares at eighty, paid out of Province 1's share. That leaves the rest of the purse for everyone else, and the crown still pays the full twenty on every square anyone names. Reina, three's a modest ask. Silke, Paddy, Rich, name yours.",
 "Clark: I've taken the four squares on the far side, the farthest I can get from that soldier at H14, since a square W3 can reach in three moves is a square I'd pay for twice. Province 1 only takes four so there's plenty left over for the others, and every square I fund for them goes in the ledger as a favour owed.")
buy_land(2, ["E11","F13","F14"], {1:60}, "New land: Reina buys three squares on Province 2's strip, E11, F13 and F14; the crown pays all 60.")
buy_land(3, ["E13"], {1:20}, "New land: Rich buys E13 on Province 3's strip; the crown pays all 20.")
buy_land(5, ["H30","I30","J30","K30","L29","M28","M29","N28"], {1:160}, "New land: Silke buys eight more squares of sea for Province 5, H30, I30, J30, K30, L29, M28, M29 and N28; the crown pays all 160.")
say('Silke', "For nine years I've asked the crown for tens and sixes because it had nothing in the drawer, Clark, and now it has five hundred and forty-five, a hundred and ninety-two of them mine. So this year I'll take your offer at its word: eight squares at twenty, the crown's full share, a hundred and sixty. That still leaves you three hundred and ten. The canal, the Irrigation and the Pagoda come to a hundred and thirty between them, so I've counted it, you can afford them, and I'll be watching to make sure each one goes up.",
 "Silke: When the crown was empty I paid for my own sea to keep the stone coming, and now the crown is fat on my tax, so I'm taking some of it back as land. The works are still covered, and every grain I don't spend is one more in my hand at the end.")
buy_land(6, ["T13","U13","V13","U22","V22"], {6:90, 1:10}, "New land: Paddy buys five more squares for Province 6, T13, U13, V13, U22 and V22, paying 90; at his own asking the crown pays 10, two a square.")
say('Paddy', "Five more squares, Clark: three up the north end and two beside Rob's lad at U21, so he's got neighbours. I'll pay ninety and the crown pays two a square, because that purse has a canal, an irrigation and a Pagoda still to find, and I'd like the record to say Province 6 paid its own way. Reina, write it down like that.",
 "Paddy: It's a fat year and nobody can raid the coast, so it's a good year to put spades in. I'll not take the crown's whole twenty when there's two hundred and ten of work still owed, and I'd rather it went into stone than into my fields.")
act("Storyteller's note, to the table: U21 holds Rob's Customs House; his nearest soldier to it, W2, stands on Q21. And after the Reservoir and every square bought this year, the crown's reserve is 155.5 before the salaries.", map_hl=[1,6,8])
act("The auction: the Harvest Pile's own lot, the Wooden Bowl Reina sold back last year. Paddy and Rob pass.", map_hl=[2,3,5])
say('Reina', "That Bowl sat on G14 under my roof until Rob's lad bled it back into the pile for two grain. I'll pay to bring it home, but not anything I like. Silke, you've nine hundred in the drawer and eight new squares; leave a poor Official her own crockery.",
 "Reina: Twelve is the most I'll spend; with thirty-three grain I need something left for whatever the next population card costs me. If Silke wants it badly enough, she can have it, and the pile gets fatter for the Pagoda.")
say('Rich', "The Bowl's back in the pile, and I won't pretend I don't miss it. Rob's lad still owes me one. I'll bid on it honestly, but I'm not paying silk prices for clay, Silke. Three quarters of whatever we bid goes straight into the pile, and I'd sooner see that grain in your Pagoda, Clark.",
 "Rich: A Bowl with my Jar makes good leverage, but Rob still has my Customs House, so it's no ladder. I'll go to twelve, and if Silke wants to drown it in silk, she's welcome to. That leaves me grain for a sword.")
say('Silke', "I'll correct my own count before anyone else does: the crown's on a hundred and fifty-five and a half, not three hundred and ten, and with a hundred and thirty of stone still owed it's tight. So next year the coast asks for less, Clark, you have my word on that. As for the Bowl, I'm not throwing grain into the pile over a piece of wood, but Rich, don't assume it'll come cheap.",
 "Silke: I don't need that Bowl, so I'll only bid three of my own for it, and I'll go to fifteen if that's what it takes to keep it out of my landlord's hands. Nobody at this table gets a set built out of my rent.")
say('Paddy', "No Bowl for me. I've not got a piece on the board and I sleep better for it. And Silke, it's a hundred and fifty-five in the drawer, not three hundred. That's still enough for the hundred and thirty of works if nobody gets greedy, so I'd ask everyone to go gently on that purse next year, me included.",
 "Paddy: A bowl's no use to me without a House, a Deed and a Jar to go with it, and Rob's got all three of those. I'll keep my grain for tax and for land, where it's doing some good.")
say('Rob', "I'll sit this one out. I've already got a Bowl on my sand, and I paid nothing for it. Reina, go on, buy it back. It's a lovely thing to have about the house, and you've got thirty-three in your pocket. I'll even promise to admire it.",
 "Rob: A Bowl I buy at auction does nothing for my list. A Bowl Reina buys is a Bowl I can raid, and W3 is still standing at H14 with nothing to do.")
lot(PILE, "WB", [B(5,3), B(5,None,"Keep it, then. It's one Bowl, and I've got eight squares of sea."), B(3,11), B(2,12)], hover="The Harvest Pile's own lot, minimum 2; the pile keeps the whole price. Silke went to 3. Reina and Rich both stood at 12, and Reina led the tie on seat order; in one live raise Reina went to 15 and Rich passed. Reina wins at 12 and puts the Wooden Bowl on J16.", at="J16")
say('Reina', "Fifteen, Rich, and not a grain more. You paid your quarter on the Wall and I'll grant you that, but this Bowl sat under the stone I asked for first. If you want it more than that, take it, and the pile will thank you both.",
 "Reina: Fifteen leaves me eighteen, which is about the least I'll keep against a bad year. If Rich goes past it, I'd rather lose a Bowl than sit in debt again.")
say('Rich', "Twelve was my figure, Reina, and I don't go past my own figure. The Table's seen that before. Take your Bowl home, and may it sit longer under your roof than it did last time. Don't let Rob's lad near it.",
 "Rich: Twelve was the most I meant to pay, and Reina with a Bowl and no Jar and no Customs House is no threat to anybody. I keep my hundred and thirty-nine. That's a sword's worth of grain, and the Reservoir starts paying me next year.")
say('Reina', "It goes on J16, out in the far corner of Province 2, where Rob's lad would have to walk a long way for his five. Thank you, Rich. That's twice this year you've behaved like a neighbour, and I've written that down as well.",
 "Reina: J16 is out of every soldier's reach this year, and I'll have the summer to watch where W3 walks next. One piece back on the board and twenty-one grain in my pocket: poor, but not in debt, and that's the line I won't cross again.")
march({"W1":"J9", "W3":"I15"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto J9 in Rich's Province 3, raiding 5 from Rich, one step from his Dragon Jar on I8; W3 steps onto I15 in Reina's Province 2, raiding 5 from Reina, one step from her Wooden Bowl on J16. W2 stands on Q21: Rob's disaster bars any raid on the coast this year.")
say('Rob', "Reina, you put it in the far corner, and that was thoughtful of you. My lad's a good walker, though. That's five from you tonight and five from Rich, and both with my compliments. Rich, your Jar's looking lonely up there by the new Reservoir. Silke, Paddy, Heaven's keeping my hands off you this year, so enjoy the quiet.",
 "Rob: W3 is one step off Reina's Bowl at J16 and she still has sixteen in hand, so this time she can't sell it out from under me before I get there. W1 is sitting next to Rich's Jar, just to keep him awake at night.")
salaries({2:10}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Reina 10 as the Official, as declared.")
act("Clark pays Silke 3 for reading the I Ching.", deltas={pid(1):-3, pid(5):3}, deal=True, map_hl=[1,5])
act("Year 11 ends with the Province 3 Reservoir built, twenty-one more squares of new land across all five provinces, the Wooden Bowl back on Reina's J16, 142.5 in the crown's reserve, and 130 in works still owed: the Province 1 canal, the Province 2 Irrigation System and the Marble Pagoda.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
# ============================== YEAR 12 ==============================
R(12, "Steady Weather", "winds")
act("Year 12 opens. Silke reads the I Ching again as the crown's appointee.", map_hl=[1,5])
act("Silke reads the I Ching: Steady Weather. Every province collects an average harvest; the population stays at 200 million, x2. No disasters.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say('Silke', "\"Steady Weather. Neither feast nor famine. Nobody writes poems about years like this.\" An average harvest in every province, no disasters for anybody, and the counter's still at two hundred million, times two, so read the same row again. Clark, it's Year 12, and on your own page that's the Province 1 canal. There's a hundred and forty-two and a half in the drawer and a hundred and thirty owed, so this is the year we find out whether the ink holds.",
 "Silke: A dull year suits a coast with thirty-eight squares, and I won't need a good harvest when I've got that much land. What I'm watching is whether Clark digs his own ditch this year, and how much of it he asks the rest of us to pay for.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say('Rich', "Steady Weather, and the pile pays out steady. A hundred and seventy-five to the crown, seventy-five to Reina, seven hundred and seventy-five to Silke, five hundred and ninety-five to Paddy, and a hundred and seventy-five to Province 3's new fields. Rob, you owe the pile ten this year, and I'll have it, thank you. Rent's the customary five, ladies and gentlemen, the same as last year and the year before. And Rob, my Jar isn't lonely. It's got a Reservoir for company now, and it'll have a sword beside it soon.",
 "Rich: Rob's lad on J9 is one step from my Jar, so this year's grain goes on a soldier before anything else. With three hundred and nineteen in the granary I can afford one, and still have plenty left over.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("The crown's year: Clark declares a tax of 20%. His one work is both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 1, 80, the crown paying all of it. On new land the crown again pays the full 20 on every square, and Province 1 buys three squares of its own. The Official's salary is 10, and Silke gets 3 for the reading.", map_hl=[1])
say('Clark', "Silke, the ink holds. The Province 1 canal goes in this year, both sections, eighty out of the crown's own drawer and nobody else's, exactly where my page put it. The coast asked me to go gently, so the rate drops to twenty, which is three hundred and twenty-four, and the crown still pays the whole twenty on every new square. Next year it's Reina's Irrigation, then the Pagoda on its own, and then there's nothing left on the list.",
 "Clark: I'm cutting the rate in a steady year when I have the stone money already, and that's a favour Silke and Paddy will remember when it counts. Two works left, and I've kept every square I buy well clear of that soldier creeping toward G17.")
taxes(.20, skip=(8,), collector="Reina, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal in Province 1, both sections", 80, {1:80}, canal={1:2}, text="Public works: both sections of the Grand Canal go in through Province 1, the crown paying all 80.", hover="80 into the Harvest Pile. Each section adds 10 to Province 1's harvest from next year. Two works are still owed: the Province 2 Irrigation System and the Marble Pagoda, 50 in all.")
buy_land(1, ["E25","E26","F28"], {1:60}, "New land: Clark buys three more squares for Province 1, E25, E26 and F28, the crown paying all 60.")
say('Reina', "Three hundred and twenty-four, counted twice: Silke one fifty-five, Paddy one nineteen, Rich thirty-five, and my own fifteen first, as always. Clark, I'll strike the Province 1 canal off when the water's in it. That leaves the Irrigation in Province 2 next year, and I've already written Year 13 beside it in my own hand. Two squares for me, the crown's twenty each. I'll ask modestly, the way the coast has.",
 "Reina: Two free squares under the Wall I asked for first will grow my harvest without touching my eighty-six. Rob's lad is one step from my Bowl again, so I'll want grain in my pocket rather than in the pile.")
buy_land(2, ["E14","E15"], {1:40}, "New land: Reina buys two more squares on Province 2's strip, E14 and E15; the crown pays all 40.")
say('Rich', "Twenty percent, a canal paid out of the crown's own drawer, and the stone going in on the very year it was promised. Clark, I flattered you when you were strong, and now I'm only telling the truth. I'll have E14 if Reina leaves it to me. And Rob, here's twenty for one of your lads. You must sell, and I must say it's the best five you've earned off me in years.",
 "Rich: W1 is one step from my Jar, and my soldier goes in beside it, so from next round I've a man standing on the Jar and Rob's lad has nothing to take. Twenty for a sword is cheaper than another thirty-six for a Jar.")
act("Rich and Reina both named E14, Province 3's only open square this year; Province 2 comes first in table order, so E14 is Reina's, and Rich buys no land.", map_hl=[2,3])
sell_soldiers([(3, "I8")], "Rich buys a soldier from Rob for 20 (Rob keeps 5, 15 to the Harvest Pile). It stands on I8, on his Dragon Jar, guarding it.", hover="Province 3 has been raided, so Rich's soldier may move from next round. A Warlord soldier must capture it straight on before it can take the Jar.")
say('Rich', "E14's yours, Reina. Good neighbours make a good Wall, and I said so first. Rob, my lad stands on the Jar itself. Your lad on J9 can admire it from there, the way you promised to admire Reina's Bowl.",
 "Rich: He stands right on the Jar, so it's guarded from tonight, and the soldier one step away gets nothing. Losing E14 cost me nothing, since the crown was paying for it anyway, and I keep two hundred and sixty-four in the granary.")
buy_land(5, ["L30","M30","N29","N30","O27","O28"], {5:108, 1:12}, "New land: Silke buys six more squares of sea for Province 5, L30, M30, N29, N30, O27 and O28, paying 108; at her own asking the crown pays 12, two a square.")
say('Silke', "The canal's going in on time, Clark, so I'll write the ink down as holding. And I said last year the coast would ask for less, so here's what that looks like: six squares, two a square from the crown and the other hundred and eight from me. That leaves the drawer what it needs for Reina's Irrigation and the Pagoda, with room to spare.",
 "Silke: I took the crown's whole twenty last year and then had to correct my own count in front of everyone, so this year I'm paying for my own sea. It's six more squares at twenty a year each, and they'll have paid for themselves before the Pagoda goes up.")
buy_land(6, ["S12","T12","U12","V12","U23"], {6:90, 1:10}, "New land: Paddy buys five more squares for Province 6, S12, T12, U12, V12 and U23, paying 90; at his own asking the crown pays 10, two a square.")
say('Paddy', "There's the canal in, Clark, and the rate down besides. That's two figures kept in one year, and I'll say so out loud. Five squares from me again: four across the top and one tucked in by Rob's Jar. I'll pay ninety and the crown pays its two a square, so there's plenty left in the drawer for Reina's Irrigation and the Pagoda.",
 "Paddy: The crown kept its word, so I'll keep mine and pay my own way. The less I take out of that purse, the surer I am the last two works go up on time.")
act("No auctions this year: every province's stock is sold, and the Harvest Pile holds no piece.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
march({"W1":"J10", "W2":"P21", "W3":"J16"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto J10 in Rich's Province 3, raiding 5 from Rich; W2 steps onto P21 in Silke's Province 5, raiding 5 from Silke; W3 steps onto J16 in Reina's Province 2 and carries off her Wooden Bowl to M16 in the desert.", take={"W3":("WB","M16")})
say('Rob', "Reina, I did promise to admire it, and I'll admire it every day now. It's coming home to the sand with me, to M16, next to the first one. Silke, Heaven's let go of my wrists, so that's five from the coast, the first you've ever paid me. Rich, a fine guard you've bought there. He's my old lad, so mind you feed him. W1 will take his five off your fields instead.",
 "Rob: That's Reina's piece taken at last, a Bowl of my own, honestly raided. Only Silke's left on my list, and W2 has just walked into her fields to wait for her first purchase.")
act("Storyteller's note, to the table: it isn't Silke's first: W2 raided 5 from her at P25 in Year 1.", map_hl=[5,8])
salaries({2:10}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Reina 10 as the Official, as declared.")
act("Clark pays Silke 3 for reading the I Ching.", deltas={pid(1):-3, pid(5):3}, deal=True, map_hl=[1,5])
act("Year 12 ends with both sections of the Province 1 canal built, sixteen more squares of new land, Reina's Wooden Bowl on the Warlord's desert, Rich's soldier guarding his Jar, 426.5 in the crown's reserve, and 50 in works still owed: the Province 2 Irrigation System and the Marble Pagoda.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
# ===== CHAPTER 7: The Last Line of the Book
# ============================== YEAR 13 ==============================
R(13, "A bad harvest", "dribble3")
act("Year 13 opens. Silke reads the I Ching again as the crown's appointee.", map_hl=[1,5])
act("Silke reads the I Ching: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest; the population stays at 200 million, x2. No disasters.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say('Silke', "Bad Harvest. \"The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame.\" Every province takes a bad harvest, mine included, so nobody gets let off. The population holds at two hundred million, times two, so read the x2 row and the bad-harvest column, and I'll be counting along with each of you. Fifty of stone still owed, Clark. A lean year is still a year, and Reina's Irrigation doesn't care what the rains did.",
 "Silke: A bad year hits the coast like it hits everyone, but forty-four squares still pay out in a bad year, and that's why I bought them when everyone else was frightened. What I'm watching now is whether the crown's drawer can still lay stone in a lean year, or whether it comes asking the coast to cover it.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say('Rich', "Bad harvest, so the pile pays out as the book says, and I'll count every grain of it into your hands myself. The rent's the customary five from Silke and five from Paddy, the same as Year 1 and every year since, thirteen of them now. And Silke, you'll be wanting to tell me I'm squeezing you in a lean year. You're sitting on two thousand three hundred. I could ask more, and you know I won't.",
 "Rich: Thirteen years at five, and that number is worth more to me than any increase, because when the last stone goes up nobody at this table will be able to say the Gentry broke his word. The land behind the Wall and the Reservoir is what fills my granary now, so I don't need to squeeze the peasants for it.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("The crown's year: Clark declares a tax of 20%. His one work is the Province 2 Irrigation System, 30, the crown offering to pay all of it. On new land the crown pays the full 20 on every square; Province 1 buys none this year. The Official's salary is 10, and Silke gets 3 for the reading.", map_hl=[1,2])
say('Clark', "Twenty percent, the same as last year. I won't squeeze a lean field, Silke, and you can write that next to the rest. Reina, your Irrigation goes in this year, the year you wrote down yourself, and the crown pays every grain of the thirty. You've paid for stone over your own roof twice, so this one is on the drawer. Land is the full twenty a square again, from me. And Rob, before you ask: the figure is still nothing. That leaves one line in the book, the Pagoda, next year, standing on its own.",
 "Clark: Six hundred and fifty in the drawer and thirty to spend, so I can afford to be generous, and in a bad year generous is the cheapest friendship there is. After this only the Pagoda is left, and I mean to be sitting here when it goes up.")
taxes(.20, skip=(8,), collector="Reina, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Province 2 Irrigation System", 30, {1:30}, key="irr2", at="G13:G14", text="Public works: the Province 2 Irrigation System goes in on G13 and G14, the crown paying all 30, with Reina's agreement.", hover="30 into the Harvest Pile. The Irrigation adds 10 to Province 2's harvest from next year, and a Warlord soldier crossing it pays a toll. One work is still owed: the Marble Pagoda, 20, in a year when nothing else is built.")
buy_land(2, ["D15","D16","E16","F15","F16"], {1:100}, "New land: Reina buys five more squares on Province 2's strip, D15, D16, E16, F15 and F16; the crown pays all 100.")
say('Reina', "Three hundred and fifty-six, Clark, with my own eighteen counted in first, as always. The rest is counted twice and comes to you whole, lean year or not. I'll take the Irrigation, and gladly: G13 and G14, right under the Wall, which, I'll remind everyone, was my idea eleven years before anyone here would pay for a stone of it. I'm writing 'settled' beside Year 13 in my own hand. I'll take the five open squares as well, D15, D16, E16, F15 and F16, on the crown's twenty, since the drawer can spare it and Province 2 has gone hungry long enough. After this there's one line left, Clark. The Pagoda, next year. Don't let it slip.",
 "Reina: Water on my own fields while I'm still the one sitting in this chair, and the crown pays for every grain and every square. I've waited thirteen years for this. G13 and G14 sit out of reach of Rob's lads, because I'm not giving him a third thing of mine to admire from his sand.")
act("Rich has no square open to him this year: Province 3's strip below the Wall is all bought.", map_hl=[3])
buy_land(5, ["F29","F30","G30","O29","O30","P27","P28","P29","Q26","Q27"], {1:200}, "New land: Silke buys ten more squares of sea for Province 5, F29, F30, G30, O29, O30, P27, P28, P29, Q26 and Q27; the crown pays all 200.")
say('Silke', "Ten squares, Clark, and this year I'll take your twenty a square at its word: two hundred from the drawer, nothing from me. That still leaves the crown about seven hundred and seventy, and the Pagoda costs twenty, so nobody can say the coast stood between Heaven and its last stone. And Rich, five is five, and yes, I counted it. Thank you for not asking more in a lean year. Write that down, it's the first time I've said it.",
 "Silke: For two years I asked the crown for less because its drawer needed the grain for stone. Now there's twenty of work left and nearly a thousand in the drawer, so every square the crown pays for is grain that stays in mine, and grain in hand is how the coast keeps score at the end.")
buy_land(6, ["T11","U11","V11","U24","V23"], {6:90, 1:10}, "New land: Paddy buys five more squares for Province 6, T11, U11, V11, U24 and V23, paying 90; at his own asking the crown pays 10, two a square.")
say('Paddy', "Five more, Clark, and the same as the last two years: ninety from me and ten from the drawer, the crown's two a square. I thank you for the full twenty, I do, but keep it for the Pagoda. If next year's the last line in the book, I want it written beside it that Province 6 paid its own way to the end. And here's my tax for Reina, every grain of it. Count it slow, Reina, I'll be watching you do it.",
 "Paddy: Even if the game ends next harvest, each of those squares pays its twenty back once, so it costs me nothing, and the crown keeps its purse for the last stone. I've nothing on the board for Rob to carry off, and I'd like it to stay that way right up to the Pagoda.")
act("No auctions this year: every province's stock is sold, and the Harvest Pile holds no piece.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
march({"W1":"K10", "W2":"P20", "W3":"I17"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto K10 in Rich's Province 3, raiding 5 from Rich; W2 steps onto P20 in Silke's Province 5, raiding 5 from Silke; W3 crosses the north river into Clark's Province 1 at I17, paying the crown a 5 toll and raiding 10.")
say('Rob', "Nothing, Clark? Then you won't mind me paying your river toll like an honest traveller, five in, and taking ten back out of Province 1 for the walk. W3 can see your palaces from here, by the way. Lovely roofs. Silke, another five from your barns, and the minute you put a piece down, my lad will be standing on it. Rich, five, as usual. Don't get up.",
 "Rob: Bad harvest, fifty-eight and a half in the purse, and one line left in Clark's book, so every lad eats tonight and W3 goes and leans on the Emperor's fence. I don't want his palaces broken. I want him to feel it every round until he names a figure, and I want to be standing in Silke's barns when she slips.")
salaries({2:10}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Reina 10 as the Official, as declared.")
act("Clark pays Silke 3 for reading the I Ching.", deltas={pid(1):-3, pid(5):3}, deal=True, map_hl=[1,5])
act("Year 13 ends with the Province 2 Irrigation System built on G13 and G14, twenty more squares of new land, a Warlord soldier standing in Province 1 at I17, 654.5 in the crown's reserve, and one work still owed: the Marble Pagoda, 20.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
# ============================== YEAR 14 ==============================
R(14, "The public utility tax", "utility")
act("Year 14 opens. Silke reads the I Ching again as the crown's appointee.", map_hl=[1,5])
act("Silke reads the I Ching: the Utility Tax. Every province collects an average harvest; the population stays at 200 million, x2. Every province pays the Emperor 2, and 2 more for each province with a public work. No disasters.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say('Silke', "Utility Tax. \"A new levy, for the upkeep of things you may or may not have.\" It's an average harvest everywhere. Every province pays the crown two, and two more if it has a public work, so Provinces 2, 3, 5 and 6 pay four each and Rob's desert pays two. That's eighteen to the drawer. Population holds at two hundred million, times two, so read the x2 row, average column. And I'll say it plainly, Clark: I'm paying two grain a year to keep a canal standing that I already paid for twice. Put that eighteen toward the Pagoda and we'll call it even.",
 "Silke: Four grain is nothing out of two thousand, but the coast doesn't let any levy go by uncounted, not even the last one. The drawer has six hundred and fifty-odd and the Pagoda costs twenty, so this is the year to make sure that stone goes in and the book gets closed.")
act("The Utility Tax: Reina, Rich, Silke and Paddy each pay the crown 4 (their provinces all have public works), and Rob pays 2; 18 to the crown.", deltas={pid(2):-4, pid(3):-4, pid(5):-4, pid(6):-4, pid(8):-2, pid(1):18}, map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say('Rich', "An average year, so the pile pays out by the book, and I'll count it into every hand myself. Silke, I'll take your thanks and frame it. The rent is the customary five from each of you, the same as Year 1, and fourteen years now without a grain more. If the Pagoda goes up next year, nobody at this table can say the Gentry raised it on his way out the door. And Rob, five again? You've eaten more of Province 3 than its own rice ever did.",
 "Rich: Raising it now would gain me a few grain and lose me fourteen years of keeping my word, and I'd rather end the night known as the man who never broke a figure. My fields behind the Wall and beside the Reservoir earn me more than any squeeze would, so I'll keep the grain they bring in and the reputation with it.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
act("Storyteller's note, to the table: the Pagoda is owed this year, Year 14, the only work left.", map_hl=[1])
act("The crown's year: Clark declares a tax of 20%. His one work is the Marble Pagoda, on G22 in Province 1, the crown paying all 20, with nothing else built beside it. It is the last work owed.", map_hl=[1])
say('Clark', "Twenty percent, the same as the last two years, average harvest or not. Reina, you'll want this line for your ledger: the Marble Pagoda goes up this year on G22, between my two palaces, the crown paying the whole twenty, with nothing else built beside it. Silke, your eighteen goes into it, so call it even, as you asked. That's every work this table ever asked of me: the Wall, three canals, the Reservoir, the Irrigation and now the Pagoda, each one written down first and built after. Rob, you can admire the roofs from I17 like everyone else. The figure is still nothing.",
 "Clark: Fourteen years of the ledger, and the last line is struck off by the man who wrote it, and the chair stays exactly where it is. I'm keeping the twenty a square because the land terms are in the book too, and I'm not breaking a promise on the last page.")
taxes(.20, skip=(8,), collector="Reina, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say('Reina', "Four hundred and fifty-eight, Clark, with my own forty-five counted in first, as I've done every year I've held this bag. Paddy, I counted yours slowly, as you asked, and it's all there. Rob owes nothing and pays nothing, and I've written that down too. So that's the last line in the book: the Marble Pagoda on G22. Put it up, Clark, and I'll strike it off in my own hand with 'settled' beside it. Just let the record show the Wall over the north was my idea eleven years before this crown would pay for a single stone of it.",
 "Reina: Every grain delivered whole, the Irrigation watering my own fields, and the last work goes up with me sitting in the Official's chair. Clark gets his Pagoda, but everyone at this table knows whose Wall stands over the north.")
build("Marble Pagoda", 20, {1:20}, key="Marble Pagoda", at="G22", text="Public works: the Marble Pagoda goes up on G22 in Province 1, the crown paying all 20, with nothing else built this year.", hover="20 into the Harvest Pile. It is the last public work owed: every work in the occupied provinces now stands, so the dynasty survives and the game ends here (Section 18), before the auction, the march and the salaries.")
act("The game ends: every public work owed is built, and the dynasty survives. Clark ends on the throne with 1,365.5 in the crown's reserve.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])

# ============================== THE END ==============================
RESULT = export(
 "Survival (Section 18): every public work owed was built in Year 14. Players are ranked by the seat they finished in against the one they drew, with grain breaking ties.",
 ["Clark drew the Official and finished on the throne. He won a complete set in Year 1 and never used it on the throne; he backed the revolt in Year 2, claimed the throne through four years of Chaos on the strength of his friends and the works he put into the record, and was acclaimed by all five players in Year 6. From then on he built a work every year: the Winter Palace, the canals in Provinces 6, 5 and 1, twelve links of the Great Wall in two years, the Reservoir, the Irrigation and the Pagoda. He paid a share of every square of land, and in his last years cut the tax to 20%.",
  "Silke drew the Silk Peasant and held it, the richest player at the table with 2,991.5. She called the revolt that stood in Year 2, with Rob, backed Clark from the first count to the last, and bought 54 squares of sea, most of them half-paid by the crown. She counted every grain she paid out loud.",
  "Paddy drew the Rice Peasant and held it, with 2,170.5. He backed the revolt, stood aside from every count until Year 6, when he put a public deadline on 'a real figure for a canal' and moved first to back Clark. Province 6 got the first canal of the reign, and by the end Paddy was paying nearly all of each square himself (39 in all) so the record would say Province 6 paid its way.",
  "Rich drew the Gentry and held it, with 530. He backed the revolt, led the acclamation in grain for four years, and dropped his claim to back Clark in Year 6. He won the Jar that would have let him take the throne straight back, held it, and called it something that served the reign; Province 3's Wall links and its Reservoir carry his share.",
  "Rob drew the Warlord and held it. He refused the tax, called the revolt, claimed the throne on his soldiers through all four years of Chaos, and carried seven pieces off into the desert. His objective stopped one name short: Silke never put a piece on the board.",
  "Reina drew the throne and lost it, finishing as the Official with 354. She taxed half of every harvest for two years to build the Summer Palace and plan the Wall, answered the revolt with pride and no cut, and sat deposed beside a sealed treasury for four years. Acclaiming Clark, she took his old seat, collected his tax, and placed the Irrigation she had always wanted under the Wall she had asked for first."])
